European inflation slipped into food
Accelerated inflation, recorded in the euro area, is mainly due to dizzying growth in the price of foodstuffs.
The increase in prices in the month of November has been higher than expected in the 13 countries of the euro area, registering - after 2.6 per cent in October - an annual inflation of 3.1 per cent - the Luxembourg-based Union Statistical Office announced.
"Official forecasts were only 3 per cent likely," the Bloomberg announced.
The European Central Bank (ECB), unlike U.S., British and Canadian issuing banks, did not cut its core interest, on the pretext that the decline, coupled with rising prices of raw materials and the reduction of forced unemployment, would lead to a spiral of inflation.
The ECB wants to keep the rate of inflation below but at least about 2 per cent.
According to an analyst, the ECB is in trap 22: "it is necessary to negotiate" on inflation so that it does not subsequently have to intervene on this issue.
Inflation of 3.3 per cent, recorded in Germany, broke the 12-year record, while in Spain it increased from 3.6 per cent to 4.1 per cent.
Inflation skyrocketed in the euro area due to galloping food and energy prices.
Wheat quotations increased by 88 per per cent and the soybean quotation also reached its highest level since 1973.
In the same way, much more had to be paid in 2007 for vegetable oil and for dairy products.
The price of crude oil on the global market increased this year by 52 per cent, already reaching black gold last month almost $100 a barrel.
According to the ECB, inflation in 2008 will jump the current 2.1 per cent to 2.5 per per cent, on the other hand by 2009 it will already fall to 1.9 per cent.
According to analysts, annual inflation will be maintained in the next 3 or 4 months at around 3 per cent, however the average annual rate will be 2.1 per cent.
At the same time, it is expected that during 2008 the ECB will reduce interest rates twice.
Gallup foresees a government crisis
After a lengthy stalemate in early December, support from the Alliance of Young Democrats increased significantly, reaching its highest level since 2002, while the Gallup never before experienced such a low level - 13 per cent - for the socialists.
The difference in the case of secure party constituents - due to the greater firmness of opposition voters - would mean support of more than two-thirds (71 per cent) compared to one-fifth support for socialists (20 per per cent).
According to the poll, the Democratic Forum and the Alliance of Free Democrats wouldn't even reach the necessary limit to have some parliamentary escano: their support among the entire population is two percent.
Among secure voters there are one per per cent more sympathizers on both sides.
The economic mood continued to deteriorate even compared to the previous lowest point - it has never been so high, 41 per cent the proportion of those judging the country &apos; s economic situation, while another 46 per cent believe that the situation is quite bad.
The families, after the most hopeful expectations of spring and summer, are now more pessimistic about changing their own economic situation.
As far as the next year is concerned, the majority of the population has the deterioration of the situation and only a tenth expects an improvement.
In the month of December, 71 per cent of those consulted, higher than any previous Gallup survey, estimated that the President of the Government is rather uncomfortable with his functions.
Thiteen per cent of those consulted rely or rely heavily on Ferenc Gyurcsány, while Viktor Orbán is supported by 38 per cent of those consulted.
54 per cent of the population does not trust the head of government at all and 27 per cent do not trust the president of the Young Democratic Alliance.
One of the indicators of shaping the political situation is that, what proportion of society think it is possible to control the discontent experienced against the person of the Head of Government without a change of government.
While 35 per cent after the riots of October last year felt it would be desirable to keep the Government and the Head of Government in power, this proportion is currently only 20 per cent, while the relative majority (33 per cent) believes that the only solution is the holding of new elections.
An interest in the referendum is characterized by the fact that 60 per per cent of those surveyed to the question about turnout replied that they would surely participate and another 20 per cent said they are likely to participate.
By comparison, in "a general election to be held this Sunday," only 55 per cent of the population would surely participate and another 15 per cent believe their turnout is likely. Based on the current intentions of the electors there is no doubt about the outcome of the popular consultation.
74 per cent of the population would disapprove the payment of school fees, 79 per per cent for the medical consultation service and 84 per per cent for hospital care.
If we look at these proportions only among the safe participants in the referendum, 81, 84 and 89 per cent of the voters would reject the introduction of school fees, the payment for medical consultations and that of hospitable care.
The most expensive is the Auchan, the Tesco has quintified its prices
Hyper-market hypermarkets from the commercial gold triangle of Budars continued the average increase in the average price tag by 16 per cent, with the difference that VAT has not been increased this year.
Napi Gazdaság Article
This year also increased by an average of 16 per cent in the commercial gold triangle of Budars the usual Christmas basket of Napi diary, but in all hypermarkets there was a growth much higher than Thousand HUF.
As in December last last year prices had already "shooted" in food supermarkets, so there was no dramatic percentage increase compared to the high starting point.
However, it is obviously terrifying for consumers the fact that what last year they were able to buy at a price well below the 9,000 HUF, today they can't even do for ten thousand.
Napi &apos; s basket in the hypermarkets studied surpassed the psychological limit of ten thousand HUF with a minimum of 91 HUF.
And the highest-value basket was 10 573 HUF.
(in 2003 that basket cost 6800 HUF.)
The price of liquor Unicum and fish remained almost at the level of the previous year, but there was a sharp increase in turkey, walnut breast steak and poppy seed.
Wheat and bread's flour prices followed data from the Central Bureau of Statistics, although in the Auchan we have also found a kilo of bread for the price of 139 HUF.
Competition did not reduce the price of the product to less than 200 HUF.
It was also surprising in this store that the Coca-Cola price with two-litre redeemable bottle cost less than 200 HUF, so that this product, even with the price of the bottle, cost only 257 HUF.
However, in Napi Auchan's basket it was the most expensive, while Cora was the cheapest.
According to our calculations, compared to last year's Christmas prices, Cora rose by 15, while Tesco and Auchan with about 18 per per cent the prices listed in the Napi basket.
Comparing last year &apos; steadfather basket values to that of the cheapest basket of this year, the difference has been 12 per cent, while growth between last year &apos; s closest and the most expensive this year has been 21 per cent.
The average growth was HUF 1480.
Last last year the average price growth was 1206 HUF, so during last year's Christmas prices felt the increase in VAT.
Precisely this process that shattered trade, supermarkets were visibly unable to make decisions on the price margin to be applied for each product and to calculate the effect of changing prices on consumption.
(If the trader badly plans the speed of the circulation of a product, he or she may lose a significant profit in the margin that conforms to the sales volume.)
The increase in Napi &apos; s basket prices reached such extremes as a result of the increase in VAT, which the most expensive was higher with 1720 HUF at the value recorded in 2006, while in the cheapest the difference was only 844 HUF.
This year there were no such Extremes, as the highest price now has been 1630 HUF more than the last year and the cheapest also increased with 1284 HUF.
Compared to the two Christmas seasons, there was a catastrophic growth in prices in less than 20 months, hyper-markets grew more than 30 per cent the price of first-deprivation consumer items.
(However, this does not necessarily confirm the figures of the Central Bureau of Statistics: this office, in the case of some basic necessities, recorded a price increase of more than 30 per cent in the 12-month range.
For e.g., in addition to wheat, also in the case of egg.)
According to Napi &apos; s basket, in the latter case this seems almost impossible, because in the price of this product that it was quite expensive already last year compared to the base, barely a 10 per cent price increase was recorded in the worst case.
According to all indexes, flour suppliers have a stronger position than egg suppliers, being able to impose the drastic increase in prices even on multinationals.
Hyper-stomers don't complain about the commercial gold triangle, they constantly talk about positive results.
According to our experiences, the majority of the consumers of the three shops visited are no longer interested in prices.
Of all the products they buy large quantities, taking the preserved food by pallets.
As of Wednesday morning, the three tents formed large queues in front of the boxes because the number of customers suddenly increased.
However, we have information that inside the country the sales volumes are lower, many multinationals complain to survey institutes because the revenues fall short of their plans and not only in the case of food, but also in the case of appliances.
In many hospitals it is saved with disinfectants
This year there have been several thousand infections in hospitals first caused by the improper application of antibiotics.
Hygiene circumstances in hospitals, overcrowding beds and overburdening of staff do not favor infection control - says the department-chief of the National Epidemiology Center.
In many hospitals it saves even with disinfectants.
Not too long ago, MRSA infection has been diagnosed in the Traumatology department of János Hospital.
The presence of antibiotic resistant bacteria has been detected in a man hospitalized after a serious accident.
Patient was isolated.
The same type of contagion took place in this same hospital at the beginning of the year.
An injured person from a serious car accident was discharged from the hospital and the same patient shortly afterwards had to be returned to the hospital as a result of MRSA contagion and since then no major surgical intervention could be carried out due to the presence of the bacteria in his or her body.
Until December 10 of this year there have been 1094 cases of hospital infection caused by multi-residuous agents in the Nosocomialis Surveillance National System of Nosocomialis Surveillance," said Karolina B?r?cz, a department-braister at the National Centre for Epidemiology.
This means that there has certainly been a number of infections produced by multi-residuous agents in hospitals, of the rest there is no information.
About two-thirds of infections (683 cases) are due to MRSA, i.e. methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus.
One-third of humans wear Staphylococcus aureus (SA) bacteria in their nose without causing any disease.
Infections caused by HS (e.g. throat inflammation, tonsillitis, skin infections, wound infections) react well to antibiotics and are easy to treat.
However, once exaggeratedly or unduly applied to antibiotics, the bacteria that normally react well to antibiotics are transformed into very antibiotic resistant bacteria and quickly infected under inadequate hospital conditions.
They can cause serious hospital infections, such as injury or septicaemia infections in the first place for patients with transient or chronic immunodeficiency, to patients with the weakened organism, the elderly, or low-weight people.
According to the doctor-director of the János Hospital there is nothing special about MRSA infection, these infections are daily in hospitals.
Infection should not be confused with the epidemic, the MRSA is only absent - said Tibor Nyulasi - where it does not examine its presence.
Antibiotic resistant
"These patients are isolated, the nursing staff treat them in rubber glosses and masks and there's the right antibiotics for their treatment," Tibor Nyulasi said.
Hospitalized patients are previously screened to verify the presence of MRSA in their body, but this can't even prevent the infection in all cases.
It's unfazed to take samples.
It would be possible to prevent hospital infections of MRSA in most cases, however the current national hospital circumstances do not favor for the prevention of training and contagion with multi-residuous pathogens - says Karolina B?r.cz
Since this infection occurs mainly in hospitals and nursing homes, hospitals should examine hospitalized patients and immediately isolate the sick who wear MRSA in their body.
Microbiological sampling is not fashionable in Hungarian hospitals.
This fact is also because the National Health Fund does not finance these medical examinations.
"Most of the cases come out in the public light when MRSA infections already occur visible in the patient," said the doctor-chief department of the epidemiological center.
The European Union makes strict tasks for antibiotic application in human medicine and there are also national directives to treat infections and pathological conditions.
Antibiotic therapy needs to be determined, i.e. microbiological tests should be carried out to prevent resistance to medicines, but hospitals do not always meet this requirement.
Microbiological sampling is even less frequent in the case of walking patients or in basic care - said Karolina B?r.cz.
It is saved with disinfectants
Scientological experiments have shown that in most cases MRSA is infected from one patient to another through health personnel.
Therefore a very important role the washing of the hands, the use of gum gloves and masks play a very important role, but there are also considerable faults in hospitals in this regard.
The MRSA is infected by transmitting drops and direct contact between people, however in national hospitative circumstances it is not always possible to isolate the infected patient.
What's more, hospital wards are often crowded, beds are often very close together, although to prevent the epidemic, patients should be separated for at least a distance of 1.5 meters.
"Unfortunately it can also be experienced - added Karolina B?r.cz - that in hospitals with a less favorable economic situation it is saved with disinfectants, there are few nurses and many patients and therefore there is a greater probability for the infection.
In the opinion of Karolina, where appropriate preventive measures are complied with, fewer infections of this type can be recorded or the contagion of the pathogen can be stopped.
Government programme against MRSA
The treatment of MRSA contagion is an enormous expense for the National Health Insurance Fund, so in all the countries it is being combated.
In England, for example, there is a government programme to prevent and reduce MRSA cases.
Apparently the program succeeds, according to the data provided, MRSA infections show a downward trend in England.
The European Centre for the Prevention and Control of Diseases (ECDC) has among its priorities the prevention of multi-resilient agents (including MRSA) and hospital infections due to these pathogens.
Negative the alcohol control of the police commander who caused a deadly accident
The official expert report has been made on the case of the fatal accident caused by the police commander of the XXII district.
Exhibit the possibility of driving drunk or stun in a stunned state of the then resigned police commander of the XXII district, who on Tuesday hit and caused the death of a passer-by - Attila Morvai, a spokesman for the Attorney General &apos; s Office of the City of the Capital, said on Friday after the expert report on the case had been prepared.
The expert report confirmed the outcome of primary alcohol control which had already found that the former police commander did not consume an alcoholic berth before getting behind the steering wheel.
The then-chief of the XXII district police police station on Tuesday with his private vehicle bowling Kossuth Lajos Street in the XXII district hit a 55-year-old man, who intended to cross the street, but did not in the pedestrian-appointed area.
(This last fact, i.e. the pedestrian didn't use the zebra to cross the street, according to the Regulations of the Transit Code are not mitigating circumstances, the driver must always give way when he bent.)
Passenger lost his life after being hospitalised.
Because of what happened the district police leader resigned and requested his retirement, according to the police statement, because he was very touched by the case.
The investigation of the case is carried out by the Budapest Investigative Prosecutor &apos; s Office.
Attila Morvai also said the ex-police commander from the outset collaborated with prosecutors in charge of the investigation.
EUR 400 million more for infrastructure
The Hungarian Finance Minister János Veres and the Vice-President of the European Investment Bank (EIB) Marta Gajecka signed on Friday in Budapest three credit-limit agreements for a total value of 400 million euros to finance education, transport and environmental infrastructures, as well as the energy sector.
The European Investment Bank (EIB) approved in 2006 a budget of EUR 300 million to be financed in the Hungarian budget for transport and environmental protection projects, which will be carried out from the Cohesion Fund grant, of which the parties had previously signed a credit agreement worth EUR 100 million, the contract they have just signed refers to the use of the remaining EUR 200 million.
The credit agreement of EUR 150 million is also intended to finance the budget for educational projects.
The EIB approved a total of EUR 300 million for this objective of Hungary, on the use of the remaining EUR 150 million the decision will be taken at a later date when the credit agreement is extended.
The parties signed a contract of 50 million euros for innovations, which the budget will use mainly for the innovation and research project of the University of Debrecen.
During the talks they also covered the issue of the construction of Metro &apos; s 4th line.
An agreement was reached that by the time Hungary finalise with the EU the details of Metro's funding, they will again discuss the issue that whether or not it is necessary to amend the original contract and how to do so.
According to János Veres, this may take place earlier in the first trimester of 2008.
Since 1990, the EIB has financed a total of EUR 8.4billion projects in Hungary worth EUR 8.4 billion.
The interest of the appropriations is in line with EURIBOR and the EIB offers a grace period of 8 years, without charging a fee for keeping the credit available to the debtor.
Bulgaria, EU Real Estate Paradise
It is admirable the development of the Bulgarian real estate market - the attention of the whole of Europe turns to that Balkan country soon mentioned.
What's, where and those who shop in the EU's even cheaplier market, which will quickly become more expensive?
Analyzing the Bulgarian Re/Max Divide.
The favorite goal for real estate acquisitions in Eastern Europe is undoubtedly Bulgaria, with its cheap ski slopes and beach chalet apartments.
The mountainous regions of Bansko, Borovec, Pamprovo and Vitosa are the most favorite, while on the beaches Albena, the "Costa Dorada," the "Costa del Sol," the old Sozopol and Nesebar, but more and more people are also looking for buildings around Kavarna and Balchik.
According to Re/Max analysts in Bulgaria, sooner or later also the central area of the country - such as Veliko Tarnovo, Gabrovo, Elena, Triavna will awaken the interest of real estate developers and more and more and more people will buy real estate in the particularly inexpensive Rus area.
In the same way the capital Sofia can be attractive, the commercial and office spaces promise a rapid recovery of the investment.
Bulgaria is on the best way to become one of Europe's busiest golf paradise, which can thus be a high-protceed destination also during the other two seasons, in addition to summer and winter.
Surprising investors are therefore already making their purchases, for example around Bansko.
According to analysts, those who buy real estate in Bulgaria are also striving to change their lives and their way of living.
The majority of the customers are English, but they also come from France, Belgium, Spain and increasingly from Romania.
Maltese and Cypriot promoters are also present and there are traditionally plenty of Russian cuisines on the beaches of the Black Sea and ski paradise.
Foreign Customers can be classed in three groups - says Georgi Jankov, Re/Max Regional Director in Bulgaria.
Those who intend to be installed here buy new or renovated properties for a price of between 50 and 100 thousand euros or old houses in poor condition between 5 and 15 thousand euros, to renovate them later.
They demand good communication and access, in addition for them the proximity of a large city, the shops and usually a local doctor is important.
Buyers of chalets (Bulgaria in the month of December was included among the top 10 vacation destinations of the Germans).
Real Estate Invest Investors Build Its Built in the hope of benefiting in the medium or long term is the third group.
The price of the capital's real estate is constantly growing, as demand is increasing.
Although in the European Union Bulgaria has the lowest real estate prices, in the last five years they suffered an annual increase of 21 per cent
Rising real estate prices significantly exceed the growth of wages.
Fodor and the six for hundreds of Hungarians
According to the Minister of the Environment and Hydrology Gábor Fodor the meetings at the expert level will be concluded on Tuesday and on Wednesday after the official inauguration negotiations will continue at ministerial level on the international climate regime after 2012.
Experts and politicians from 192 countries around the world are deliberating on the Bali Route Sheath available on post-emission reduction efforts, as set out in the Kyoto Protocol until 2012.
At present, lively discussions are taking place on the issue between developing and industrialized countries.
The United States has already indicated that for the time being it does not want to negotiate on precise figures, although it tends to reach the agreement that compared to the situation of 1990, up to 20 per cent until 2020 the emissions of greenhouse gases will decrease by 25 per cent to 40 per cent.
Another major theme of the conference is that whether or not industrialized countries, in addition to reducing emissions, are willing to provide material and technological assistance to countries in need.
An agreement has also been reached on the aid to be offered to developing countries.
The support fund, as provided for in the Kyoto Protocol, will be led by a 16-member board of directors, probably within the UN Global Environment Facility (GEF) - writes the "Piac és Profit."
The Six for Hundreds of Hungary
On behalf of Hungary, Minister of the Environment and Hydrology Gábor Fodor supports the EU's efforts to preserve its cutting-edge role in global climate protection and to promote the signing of the new international agreement until 2009.
The minister stated that the EU undertakes to reduce until 20 per per cent from the 1990 level of its greenhouse gas emissions by 20 per cent, regardless of whether or not the "post-2012 agreement" will be signed and is prepared to commit to reducing even by 30 per cent if the other large developed regions of the world and developing countries are also prepared to make commitments in accordance with their responsibilities and capabilities.
Gábor Fodor pointed out that the countries signatories to the 1997 Kyoto Protocol pledged to fall by 5.2 per per cent, among which the then 15 EU Member States pledged to achieve an 8 per per cent decrease.
Hungary &apos; s commitment is to reduce with 6 per per cent the emission of gases responsible for the greenhouse effect compared to the period between 1985 and "87, which our country will meet," says the press release of the Ministry of the Environment.
There is too much at stake in Balí, since in two years &apos; time the long-running international climate policy agreement will have to be signed, because if not, its entry into force would be deferred by after 2012. In this case the reduction commitments, made in the Kyoto Protocol would not be followed immediately by a new international emission reduction system, the necessity of which was noticed in its most recent report by the IPCC, an international UN scientific partnership dealing with climate change, which won the Nobel Peace Prize.
With this, the reduction of emissions would be delayed and could not prevent an increase of more than 2oC in the Earth's average temperature, a fact that scientists say would have immeasurable and irreversiable consequences.
The British army recruits on YouTube
The British army's units in a video distributor release footage of actions filmed in Iraq and Afghanistan to popularize themselves.
First the British Air Force (RADs) took advantage of the YouTube services: special units displaced in Afghanistan since March 2006 are uploading videos of their actions against the Taliban - the BBC reported.
These footage made with mini-cameras mounted on the helmets are controlled in Kandahar's general operating body, then sent to London to be uploaded to the internet.
The political messaging is inevitable.
Already there are 139 recordings of air force soldiers who have been uploaded in this way to the Internet and their "channel" has already been seen by more than 75,000 people.
The British Ministry of Defence was registered in the spring of 2007 under the name "defence headquarters" and publishes videos of both actions perpetrated in Iraq and in Afghanistan, meanwhile the Ministry of the Community of British Nations (Commonwealth Office) and the Multinational Forces in Iraq (MNFI) have its own YouTube channel.
Operation in Afghanistan
Originally the air force had uploaded the videos with recruitment targets, but in the Ministry of Defence's view they also serve to present to the public the true objective of the British army's presence abroad.
"This type of propaganda costs practically nothing compared to the conventional television spots of recruitment," defence expert Gordon Mackenzie told the BBC, on the other hand, they inevitably have some political message, since in a specific way they justify the raison d &apos; étre of military actions determined by the government.
The U.S. prepared a video game
The army also worries about the fact that, in addition to fiercely edited and censored footage, the distributor also contains amateur videos, for example about the deaths of the soldiers: in the "official" propaganda films, a little hypocritical, the officers speak at the maximum of loss, without yet presenting the bloody part of the fighting.
In addition, the battlefields of Iraq and Afghanistan have already been uploaded to the Internet numerous videos recorded in actual operations, but the British army is only recently seizing this opportunity.
The U.S. uses a slightly different form for recruitment with video games of shooting, downloadable free of charge, called America's Army, in which in the midst of a more or less real situation the player can put himself right instead of the soldiers.
Tombo and salty cake at the ministry Christmas party
Work Minister Mónika Lamperth doesn't seem very sentimental: instead of the Christmas party, she will hold a working meeting for her colleagues who will be able to say goodbye in 2007 with salty cake and cafe.
There's a ministry that will celebrate its party with live music, in another they will create the Christmas atmosphere at the heart of public officials with tombola and a toast.
Feast, Christmas vibe, concerts and buffet - that's how the power is amused.
State offices are also preparing for Christmas.
The largest Christmas party will be held in the Ministry of Defence, but not for the Ministry's workers.
On the Christmas of the Orphans will receive gifts the children of the fallen Soldiers and for the Christmas of the Missionaries the relatives of the Soldiers who are currently carrying out missions are invited abroad. A reception will also be offered for the press.
This portfolio will allocate $25 million for the festive atmosphere at the end of year.
The National Development Agency (ANF) and the Ministry of Economics and Transport (MET) will hold a luxury feast with regard to the parties of the other portfolios
The feast of the MET will be celebrated at the House of the Future, with a total budget of 4, 320 000 HUF and of the six-hundred-hundred-flortire workers of the ministry are expected to attend.
At the festival of the wallet there will reign a Christmas atmosphere, artists will also perform and there will be a tombola draw.
ANF christmas cost 4,70,000 HUF and was held on Wednesday in the National Board of Directors of Justice (CNAJ) building with musical concerts and dinners.
Rencution of the living room cost a million HUF, the rest of the budget was spent for the more than four hundred guests.
We are informed that the workers of one of the ANF departments are also invited to another Christmas party to be held by a competition management company called the Hungarian Centre for the Economic Development of Community Funds.
For this purpose they rented the complete building of the Comedy Theater.
The information has not been confirmed by CHDE SA, since we failed to communicate with the competent person.
You can attend without a costume
The CNAJ building housed not only the feast of the ANF, but also that of the Ministry of Justice and the Police, which instead of Christmas celebrated the dance of Lucia Day on December 7.
The CNAJ did not collect anything from the use of its facilities.
Fourteen people participated in the dance.
The feast began with the minister's greeting words and after dinner there was live danceable music.
Answering [origo's] question they said: "It wasn't mandatory to wear suit or evening dress."
The portfolios of health, labour and local governments are preparing for modest celebrations.
The Ministry of Public Health will organize a program and a party for its workers in its own building with a budget of 400,000 HUF.
On the other hand, Labor Minister Mónika Lamperth does not wait for her colleagues for an official Christmas party, but for an end-of-year work meeting.
There's the participation of a hundred and eighty-eight-judgments who - as reported by the press department - will only take coffee and salty cake, so the party won't cost more than 350 000 HUF.
Minister for local government Gordon Bajnai will instead simply provide with his colleagues in the Ministry's Classroom for Local Government and Territorial Development.
The Ministry of Finance (MF) will hold a Christmas feast in its own event lounge with the participation of 436 workers.
The Christmas party will have a budget of 2500 HUF per guest - from this budget you'll be paid the reception with buffet and a toast.
Ever since János Veres is the minister,  MF workers every year are celebrating Christmas in the same way.
On the other hand, there will be no festive atmosphere, the official character will take place not only because of the ministerial discourse, but also because of the afternoon hours of its celebration.
The agriculture portfolio will offer a New Year's reception on January 20, Christmas will not be celebrated at the Ministry.
The reception will be held at Vajdahunyad Castle of the Municipal Park and, according to the portfolio, there will be a budget of 1000 HUF per person for the hundred guests.
This - according to the press department - is going to be a very simple stand-by-to-footed reception, to which will be invited only specialized diplomats, accredited in Hungary.
These receptions that are celebrated every year, begin with a brief greeting speech from the minister and conclude with a modest meal.
The Ministries of Foreign Affairs and Education and on Culture won't hold any Christmas floats - that's what's the relevant press departments told [origo].
In the press department of the environmental portfolio they were not able to report on the budget of the Ministry &apos; s Christmas celebrations.
What is known is that more than four-hundred people were invited for the feast at the Museum of Applied Arts.
Strengths are gathering from the giant strike
The League Trade Unions and the National Federation of Workers &apos; Commissions on Friday will form a strike commission to co-ordinate Monday's strike that will last for an indefinite period of time.
A protest rally will be held on Saturday, while on Monday - the day the final vote will be held on the public health law - the labor strike will begin.
Surely this action will cause great difficulties in the transport, since rail traffic will be completely halted and until 1 p.m. the workers of the Budapest Collective Transport Company will also be on strike.
On Friday afternoon the League Trade Unions will report on the details of their strike on Monday that they will hold in conjunction with the National Federation of Workers' Commissions.
There will also be a strike commission of the LIGA and the National Union of Machinists who will co-ordinate Monday's actions.
On Saturday there will be a protest rally, while on Monday the participating trade unions will go on strike indefinitely, unless an agreement is reached with the government.
This means that - on the day of the parliamentary vote on the public health law - they did not report on the date of an end to the strike.
If the National Assembly disappropriates the public health law, the trade unions will immediately withdraw their call for a strike.
On the other hand, if the law is passed, the strike committees will meet and will decide on the future.
Based on Monday's National Assembly's agenda, it is also possible that there may only be a vote on the public health law in the very early hours of the night, or at best around midnight.
As a result of the strike that will begin on Monday, there will have to be significant difficulties in the transport: on the railways you will no longer work indefinitely, in the morning you can stop a part of Budapest's collective transport and more than half the inter-urban bus transport of Volán companies.
Simultaneously in many parts of the country there will be the closure of half the roads.
Numerous trade unions and civil organizations have joined the strike, their names are constantly being updated on the league's website.
Some syndicates announced their strike for an indefinite period of time, others for defined time.
As of Thursday afternoon the State Railroad and the Free Railway Workers Union (SLTF) did not be able to reach an agreement even on the minimum services, although, as the Christmas holidays approach, more and more people are traveling on the trains.
According to information received so far, from the beginning of the day until 1 p.m. the workers of the Budapest Collective Transportation Company will be on strike from 7 a.m. to 9 a.m. will hold a four-hour strike the LIGA Syndicates of the Electricity and Thermal Energy Industry, the Educator's Trade Unions called for a strike of 2 and the Democratic Union of Educators from 2 to 8 a.m.
The Hungarian Association of Physicians will act on a national strike at alternative times and the Democratic Union of Workers in the Health and Social Sector (SDTSSS), which are part of the Forum for Trade Union Cooperation, will hold a two-hour warning strike.
The most important of the four demands made by the League is for the government to withdraw its proposal for public health reform and submit a new conception to a social debate.
The second demand is for them to gradually introduce the new form of calculation of pensions that will come into force next year.
The third is to ensure, even before 2010, in the case of advanced pensions the possibility that the occupations that deserve it can enter the system.
The fourth reason for the strike is that the Free Trade Union of Railway Workers (SLTF), which is part of the League Trade Unions, does not accept the proposal of the Ministry of Economy and Transport on the railway side lines and are expecting a different proposal.
Political Capital[b1]: In the country the risk is increased by the Husmana Guard
According to the Division of Risk Analysis (Risk Analysing Division) of the Political Capital the activity of the Hungarian Guard, formed in the month of August of this year with the support of the Movement for a Better Hungary, from several points of view the political risk of Hungary and the region has increased.
The company's "resugation of risks" writes: in relation to the activity of the Hungarian Guard the greatest political risk constitutes the fact that "with the aggravation of the ethnic tensions between the gypsy and non-gypsy population of Hungary, even in the short term there may be violent ethnic clashes."
"The main reason for this is that the political strategy of the Hungarian Guard is probably the provocation of an event of this character - added the representative of the research institute.
They stated that all this is due to one of the main misdemeanours of the protagonists of Hungarian political life: "the protagonists of public life ceded the public debate of the gypsy problem to the representatives of the radical and extremist political groups, who were able to substantiate their political strategy on the discrepancies between Gypsies and Hungarians."
This aggravates the problem, prevents normal dialogue, strengthens the extremists and increases the likelihood of the outbreak of violent conflict - they estimate.
In addition, according to the research institute, the activity of the National Guard, as a result of the great attention of the national and international average, "significantly deteriorated the image of Hungary abroad, especially with regard to the stability and democratic development of the country."
It reminds us that from the autumn of 2006 there has been an increasing publicity and encouragement for the reorganisation of the radical and far-left groups in Hungary and that since the summer of 2007 there have been numerous self-deprecating organizations that self-defense "associations of national self-defense."
"The common trait of these groups is that they define themselves as civil organizations, while they openly proclaim the training of their members for the defense - even armed - (civil defense, national defense, defense of public order), alluding to the ineptitudes of the police, of the army, i.e. of the State," they add.
They stated: all this takes place in accordance with the method it tries to give a civil presentation to different political aspirations, based on society's discontent with politics and with political parties.
To highlight the civil and the form of association in the case of these associations not only serves to avoid the legal problems of legal registration, but is at the same time also a political strategy.
According to the analysis, the activity of the Hungarian Guard is also a risk factor from the point of view of neighbourly relations.
"In the last few months, in many neighbouring states they referred to the Hungarian Guard as a potential danger, while the ultra-nationalist political figures of certain neighboring countries want to justify their own anti-Hungarian policy with the creation of the Hungarian Guard.
All this influences the political stability of the region," they added.
According to the research institute, the constitution of the Hungarian Guard is not an isolated phenomenon in the Central and East of Europe, so political risks have increased throughout the region.
They wrote that the Hurricane Guard functions in a similar way than the Bulgarian National Guard, whose constitution was announced in August 2007 by the far-left Bulgarian National Union (UNB).
The formation of the Bulgarian guard is due to the mass riots of the Gypsies that took place in one of the neighborhoods of Sofía (Krasna Poliana).
Taking advantage of this situation, the UNB decided to constitute the guard, on the grounds that the objective of the organization is "national self-defense."
In his opinion, in the last 17 years there has been "giant gigly Terror" in Bulgaria, however the state remained impotent and indifferent in the face of the facts - Political Capital recalls.
They added: "This initiative has provoked a lively discussion in Bulgarian public opinion.
As it was written, the increased risk of ethnic clashes indicates that some gypsy organizations in Bulgaria have announced that to resist, they will constitute their own guard.
Since the functioning of the Hungarian Guard has so far shown resemblance to that of the Bulgarian National Guard, it cannot be ruled out that the gypsies of Hungary also react to the threat in a similar way, organizing their own self-defense - it can be read in the warning about the risks of Political Capital.
Political Capital's research director Attila Juhász to the news agency's question said: "The risk analysis is one of the research institute's activities.
As he put it, each year publishes their risk analysis and when they experience something that can influence the change of risk, they inform the public, as was the case in this case, publishing their warning about the risks.
The party isn't always written with red numbers.
In Hungary the law precisely defines the days that are written with red numbers in the calendar and it has already become a generalized practice that around holidays - that the next year will be less - the working days are reorganized.
Not even in the EU or in North America are the festivities united.
The Hungarian laws make a difference between a national holiday, a state holiday and holidays.
The festive days are fundamentally related to religious feasts and historical ephemerides.
Let's Celebrate the New Year too: both the last day of the old year and the first day of the New Year is festive - the latter above all so you can rest those who spent celebrating the old anus.
Throughout the year we have eleven feasts written with red numbers on the calendar.
It's a national feast on March 15 in remembrance of the 1848 revolution and October 23, a day commemorating the 1956 revolution.
On August 20 we celebrated the founding of the State, on May 1, the work feast, on 1 and 2 November we remember our deceased relatives and friends.
In addition, in our two-day floats the Passover of Resurrection, the Pentecost and the Christmas are two-day floats.
According to the calendar, our state, national or religious holidays rarely coincide with a weekend, which's why it's often bridged when there's a single working day before or after the party.
In exchange for elongated weekends like this, you can't be absent from work or from the school on the previous Saturdays.
He is the labour minister who regulates in his specific decree when we have to work exactly in exchange for the day directly preceding or after the party.
This legal standard is binding on all employers and their employees, as well as for employees and public officials.
On the other hand, it is not mandatory for companies and service institutions that, due to their function, work uninterruptedly even during the festive days.
This year the order of work can be altered only around the festivities of the end of anus: from December 24 lives practically stop in the country, since according to the experiences, the majority of the people "make bridge" during the days between Christmas and the end of the year or take their vacations during these days.
The next year several of our holidays will coincide in the middle or on weekends, so there will be fewer changes in the working days than this year.
It will first change the agenda around the work feast so that then, for four successive days the workers will be able to rest.
Saturday, April 26 will be a working day, so that in return Friday 2 May it will be a festive day.
The other change is related to the feast of October 23: Saturday, October 18, we will have to work instead of Friday 24.
On the other hand, the anus that comes during the end-of-the-year feast you will be able to be rested, since from December 24 you won't work anywhere, even if the last day of the year it's a Wednesday.
Six small deterrents to bitter the lives of your colleagues
e-mails that we forget to send, loud messages that we never receive, innocent lies that happen to each other without realizing it: they are all part of our daily Folklore at work.
