When you knock the office on the phone and you say you're sick and unbearably hurt your head, you feel bad or okay, you feel you'd prefer to rest that day; think that others should do your job, your unstoppable chores, your work by end.
Don't screw with your buddy.
If you already know in advance that the next day you don't go to the office or something can happen to you, so you have to stay at home, tell them, so that your colleagues or your boss don't have to deal with your absence the next day.
Plots, giggles, phone calls
You're involved in your work, but they abruptly take the gimmick of the ripples out of it of your colleagues?
Have you unintentionally everned a phone conversation that would have been better not to listen to it?
Don't bother your mates with your rags, chants, loud conversations (less when you make them over your head), kicking, constant blasphemies, expressing your dissatisfaction aloud.
All these can be annoying, even if they're not delving into a project right now (writing, planning, organization).
You blaming others for a mistake?
Don't blame others if you haven't finished something in time, you've forgotten to send an important letter or a message.
Don't alude the chaos eventually reigning in the office or the loud noise your colleagues make. . . . . . . . . . . . . .
You have to recognize if you've made a mistake that harms others and you have to repair the misdemeanour.
Ask how you're gonna repair your foul.
Do you feel too much at home in the office?
Jogging and shoes: very comfortable room for the house, but it is not suitable for the office.
You don't see a comfortable jogging even if your occupation allows.
Don't be unravelled, respect your colleagues and yourself wearing everyday, elegant and handsome garments when your occupancy so requires.
You lose interest if you want to meet the expectations of the public.
