hvg.hu asked Balázs Zságer.
hvg.hu: After this new success the question arises: the one you are interpreting, to what extent is a music dedicated to a select audience?
Balázs Zságer: Defining a select audience for me means little.
In my opinion there are two types of castes in light music; non- mainstream and underground[b5], not alternative and pop, but commercial and candid.
Also the so-called subterranean alternative music can be boring, nonsense and it can be composed equally only for sale, which makes it commercial.
But a nice pop song can also emerge from sincere inspiration.
Listeners &apos; attitude also works in this spirit, regardless of national languages and borders.
hvg.hu: Do you see any difference between the reception of your music by the Hungarian and foreign public?
B. Zs: Abroad, especially to the west of us, light music has bigger traditions.
There not only the generations of fifteen and twenty-eight years attend the concerts, but also the oldest.
They pay much more attention to the changes that take place around them in light music, because they consider it part of contemporary culture.
hvg.hu: What role does the public have in your work?
Do you care who they are, where they're from and how many are the people listening to your music?
B.Zs: Naturally I feel good to see that more and more people are attending our gigs.
But we've never composed music for the market.
We don't compose music for some purpose, but for some reason.
The role of the public is rather felt in concerts where there is a reciprocal influence, but this is only in the inconceivable changes of the moment.
And yet I realised that if you want to meet the expectations of the public, you lose your interest.
hvg.hu: To what extent do you intend to follow Yonderboi's example?
He's working alone and made his career rather to the west of us.
B.Zs: We don't go his way, even though they sometimes try to compare our music to his.
Rather we work as a team, we rely on each other &apos; s musical presence and let it unravel.
At the very beginning it was inevitable that they will compare our works with Yonderboi's music, since many of us began our career in their group and from there embarked another way.
hvg.hu: In the list of successes of mr2 Pet-fi Wings of Love you are in the first place.
A lot of people first heard Zagar's name in relation to this song.
Did you consciously harpoken this style to a wider layer?
Zs.B.: We didn't mean to compose great hits and sell our album with these hits.
This song is more than that.
We add it to the end of the album, trying to relax after the sound orgy of the album and the heavier songs.
hvg.hu: How have you selected the six singers?
Why exactly to them - what's the main motif?
B.Zs: The idea was to create a gospel-style chorus.
Then came the idea of creating this chorus of good-voicing soloists from our own national musical setting.
That's how that choir of six women was created - that is Sena, Kutzora Edina, Judie Jay, Bori Péterfy, Enik. Hodosi [Neo] and Juci Németh - who was later baptized as Underground Divas.
hvg.hu: Did you exactly compare the song like that or did the girls change a few things?
Zs.B.: This song existed a long time ago in instrumental version and when we already had all the material on the album, we think something is still missing.
Gust the chord attracted chanting.
Even that night, I recorded the song that the girls later learned.
Everything almost got to the first try and incredibly spontaneous form.
Remind me, I was very excited then, but I naturally also needed Divas.
It wouldn't have worked so hard with my voice.
hvg.hu: Both at the concert and on the album it seems as if the girls are in the background.
There are no soloists (except Judy Jay's brief solo), they don't have a role of their own, they sing all together and no more strongly than the music, so the music doesn't just accompanies their songs.
Also on the stage they are located in the background.
Intentionally?
B.Zs: They're basically just because of technical problems.
On the other hand we didn't really want to distribute solo roles.
The song is also about it, about us forgeting for a while our ego, the one who has come from and where it comes from, let those wings, the wings of love lift us up.
I'd have wanted to get others to also feel the euphoric mood we felt when this song was born.
hvg.hu: Which one did you do the most?
Zs.B.: In the musical composition actually to none, rather when it came to mixing there were more difficult pieces.
For example the Dorian Gray's Dilemma where I'm Playing on a Concert Piano or the Rock'n 'Roll Goes To The Girls, where for a long time I didn't want the groove to work and then slowly it all got to its place like in a puzzle.
hvg.hu: What's your favorite song on the new album?
Zs.B.: This changes constantly with the concerts and because I've already "unleashed" the album.
