The Czech Republic is able to get $365 billion
The Ministers of the Government of the Czech Republic yesterday signed with representatives of the European Commission a total of nine operational programmes from which the Czech Republic can obtain 14 billion euros (approximately 365 billion kronor).
It is almost half of the total amount that the Czech Republic can obtain from Brussels for the years 2007 to 2013.
Approximately 125 billion kronor will come out of seven operational programmes for the regions.
Czech companies will be able to obtain about 100 billion kronor from the Enterprise and Innovation Programme.
Most of the money will go to transport, up to $150 billion.
The Transport Programme is the largest of operational programmes and accounts for up to 22 per cent of the resources that the Czech Republic can obtain from the fund.
This money increases the budget of the state's transport infrastructure fund, which next year will amount to 90.8 billion kronor.
The Enterprise and Innovation programme should serve entrepreneurs and companies to obtain resources in business innovation.
Companies should obtain funding for the acquisition of new technologies, for industrial research and for the implementation of more effective organisational methods.
With regard to the current use of EU funds, the Czech Republic is among the newly admitted countries that use them least.
Thirteen-year grinding for the former president of Nicaragua, the court decreed
The Nicaraguan Court of Appeal on Thursday decreed the 20-year sentence for the former president of the country, Arnaldo Alemán, who was convicted in 2003 for embezzlement of $100 billion (almost 1.8 billion kroner).
In spite of this, some time later, he was freed from prison to be placed under house arrest on his estate not far from the Nicaraguan metropolis.
It's not at all clear at the moment, according to the AP news agency that the then-chief of the Central American state between 1197 and 2002 is behind bars again.
According to the President of the Court of Appeal, Ileana Perez, it will rather depend on the prison officers if they let Alemán finish their punishment at home or if they make him return to the prison.
Forty-four years ago, the court showed how Alemán constantly transferred government money to a company account in Panama.
This society was fictitious and the account actually belonged to its closest relatives and supporters.
Some of this money ended in the fund Alemán created to finance his campaign in the 2001 elections.
