Apart from different debates before the vote on the government's vote of confidence, this time nothing dramatic has happened.
The Parliamentary Assembly, in this traditional sight although the most influential, quietly approved the new law.
It's a great paradox.
Each government begins and ends with every chapter and budget item, whether they like it or not.
The budget is an important part of the policy but it hides the power rivalry between the government and the opposition.
It is important for the media and therefore devote special attention to it.
The political discussions are much more interesting than the simple numbers.
The State Budgets of the Last Social Democracy governments suffered from three defects that for this year 2008 it is not possible to solve so easily:
The first of these is the high participation of the mandatory laws, known as the expenses of the presidents that are not only the pensions but also the social benefits that already last year were approved before the elections by the "silent" coalition of SSD and KS-M.
The second is the deficit in the economy as a result of the first defect.
The third limit is the deficit of public funding, such as the municipal and Community budget deficit.
In spite of this, the greatest risk is not in the state budget deficit.
It's public and relatively under control.
The clock pump, however, is in the deficit of the municipal and community budget.
plenty of mayors to be able to obtain other sources of investment, apart from the money of taxes, fees and loans from banks, they often issued, meaningless obligations with which they disproportionately indebted their town halls.
In the last few years they managed to pay these debts through the massive sale of energy shares and other development networks they received from the state and privatization.
They do not currently have such a remedy.
If we stuck only to the official and verified facts, we discover that:
A few years ago, our economy goes through phases of growth in the same way as the rest of the world, of what is boasted "SSD."
This was the ideal occasion for your government to put the state's finances in order.
Instead, however, the state remained indebted.
The rest of the countries, with the exception of some such as Hungary, Albania, Pakistan or Egypt, saved much more reasonably than our republic.
It is a great embarrassment that falls, above all, in the previous government.
We belong to the nations that within the global measures we are less threatened by poverty.
That's why there's no necessities of massive social programs that, unfortunately, in the last few years fucking with gigantic measures.
And although they managed to eliminate some of the incomprehensible populist expenses approved this year before the elections, if the retirement age doesn't soon change and pension reform then it's really going to be the end of any policy in this country and, governing whoever rules it will only be a simple bureaucracy.
Exaggerating a little we can say that the politicians who govern will transform themselves into civil servant, mechanic dealers of the bulk of the money from superannuation taxes and social benefits.
Therefore, this year's budget cannot be revolutionary, but it can stop the pressure of the normal expenses of the presidents' expenses.
The planned deficit of more than $70 billion by 2008 would have been absolutely unnecessary, if the last government hadn't squandered in the last ten years what they could.
We wouldn't want the ruling politicians to just become civil servants so we need to be patient and concede to this weak coalition of the government, which realizes that the state can only spend what it's able to get from taxes and fees, the reassurance to the preparation of other reforms of the laws.
This alone will make it possible for the gradual achievement of a balanced State Budget.
