The Banks Want to Solve the Crisis
The U.S. Federal Reserve Bank, the European Central Bank and the British, Swiss and Canadian Central Banks decided to fight the finance market crisis together.
More than a hundred billion dollars will be received in the Monetary Mark through tenders.
In these tenders, banks auction the interest rates for which they are able to lend money.
For example, the U.S. Fed will send 40 billion dollars to tender, and then another twenty-a-billion will be facilitated by the central banks of Europe and the Swiss.
In the wake of the 9/11 attacks, it's the first time that central banks from different states have agreed on this coordinated process to protect and reduce the consequences of the financial crisis.
"This step must solve the greatest economic problem, i.e. the continued growth of free capital shortages that banks could lend," Bernard Baumohl of the consulting company Economic Outlook Group wrote in his report for clients.
"This co-coordinated central bank action probably diminished recession in the U.S. from 65 per cent to 50 per cent," Baumohl said in his report.
The banks have already spent $60 billion on unpaid loans, especially in the United States.
Economists are still expecting billions in losses.
"Central Banks should really be scared of what's still waiting for them when they decided to take this step," said ABN Amro chief economist Kieran Davies.
"It seems that the situation is really wrong," he added.
Central banks fear that they will have the same problems of access to loans and will therefore have to lend more dearly to their customers.
This would reduce consumption and send the U.S. economy into recession with different impacts on the growth of the global economy.
The central banks thus decided, after they did not manage to reduce the so-called LIBOR rate, an inter-bank rate that is used for the lending of money in the interbank market.
This means that the banks constantly feared lending money even as the central banks of the United States, Canada and Great Britain reduced their interest rates several times during the autumn.
"It'll have the same effect as another fall in interest rates," Greg Salvaggio of Tempus Consulting told Reuters.
Some economists don't miss the fact that the step taken by the central banks contributes to igniting the crisis because it doesn't solve the fundamental problem that is the fall of the real estate market in the U.S.
Klaus: The environmentalists' debates are incredible
Global climate change mainly affects developing countries.
President Václav Klaus once again expressed himself regarding the problem of global global warming.
Criticism now arose with the closing of the UN international conference on climate change in Bali.
In Vienna, where he presented his book "Green America," he commented that environmentalists' debates are incredible.
According to Klaus it is unacceptable not only the structure of the conference that creates a bizarre mix of representatives of government and non-governmental organizations but the fundamental difference between the rhetoric used and reality.
"The debates are barely structured there, they're fire-arms of words, substitutes for the solution to today's problems," Klaus said.
The Czech president during the presentation of his book stressed that this issue is very important to him and that is why he is interested.
He also expressed his joy in discovering his book translated into German because as he said "many times I only encounter the caricature of my ideas."
Interested parties can now be informed about their various opinions.
Klaus recalled that he is not a climatologist, but that, in his opinion, he has sufficient assumptions to write a book like this.
He is an academic, with the capacity to follow and judging a specialized dissertation, has experiences with statistics and is also an economist.
At the main moment of the closure he expressed his thought that the climate is constantly changing and that its changes are normal, that there is no agreement on global warming there is no agreement among scientists, that the consequences of the current changes are not so great and serious, nor do they represent possible disasters and that attempts to influence climate development are useless, ineffective and expensively unacceptable.
In addition as an experienced man with totalitarianism he considers it unacceptable to impose unproven opinions on others, which he feels like a dangerous way of limiting the freedom of others.
Upon questions from those present he repeatedly explained that he is not against "reasonable defenders of the environment but against environmental militants who do not take into account the relationship between costs and benefits."
On the same subject Klaus spoke in Vienna two months ago at the Hayes Institute two months ago.
600 thousand people couldn't change their driver's license.
Six hundred thousand drivers missed the date and therefore did not manage to change their old driver's license whose validity expires at the end of the year.
Today is the last day when people are able to request the change so that officials give them time until the end of the year to issue the new card.
However, the Ministry does not fear that hundreds of thousands of drivers will be found on the roads after the New Year without the valid card.
According to traffic, the card hasn't been changed mainly by those who don't even want to drive anymore.
While in the villages it announces a rapid decline in the change of cards, the offices of the metropolis are full of applications and the interest in the new card has increased.
The City Council composes about 2,000 clients per day.
The queues are expected for half an hour.
Around 140,000 drivers have not yet been removed in Prague.
The issue of a new card lasts twenty days.
However, it is possible to request the release of the card within a shorter period of five days.
But in that case the driver must pay five hundred kronor.
