Cuba, the Caribbean's Most Beautiful Isle
For the visitors it's really the island of freedom.
Anything you expect from your vacation can be fulfilled, as long as it chooses correctly.
Slender and golden beaches, large waves or a quiet sea like a pond.
the palm trees and the whole enlisted with waiters offering pizzas on the beach, ice ice cream or varied cocktails.
Kitesurfing, golf and roulette.
On the island of the Caribbean Sea you can find all this and much more.
Only on an island it finds much even more and that island is Cuba, the largest and the most beautiful of all the islands of the Great Antilles.
At the time of the Spaniard, Cuba became the bastion of the conquest of America.
Columbus called it, the most beautiful place I've ever seen.
Havana became a warehouse and cargo port of the treasurers the Spaniards extracted from America and took to Europe.
Then came the American era.
The National Hotel grew in its late 20s.
It was ordered to build one of the bosses of the American mafia, Lucky Luciano, since everything that had criminality in the U.S. (and therefore prohibited) was welcomed and supported in Cuba such as drugs, gambling and prostitution.
His guests included, among others.
Winston Churchill, Ava Gardner, Frank Sinatra.
Tall buildings spawned in Havana, along wide avenues ran the cars that were a cult symbol in the 1950s of the last century.
Ernest Hemingway bought himself a farm in San Francisco de Paula, near Havana, and in Cojimar he had his yacht.
On the walls of Bodeguita del Medio they left their signature the stars of the world of spectacle and politicians from all over America.
What about today?
On the walls of Bodeguita abánense you can also leave your autograph.
In the pub of the expensive National Hotel you mix the cocktail according to your desires.
And those cars of cult of the 1950s, until today they are still operating in Havana, because those Lada and Fiat who arrived a long time ago have rusted between these wet and salty winds.
Even though Havana, especially the old neighborhood, is deteriorating markedly, there are not so many wonderful colonial monuments in the surroundings as there.
Other places are metamorphosed in beauty, e.g. Trinity.
Because tourism brings so much necessary money, its development is guaranteed.
In the last fifteen years new hotels grew not only in La Habana but also in Varadero, on the coast of the province of Holguín and in islets such as Cayo Coco, Cayo Guillermo and others.
Cuba still offers a curiosity that it is in the unique world and is highly valued by railway lovers.
Sugar cane is transported from immense plantations to narrow-lanft sugar factories in steam or electric machines.
Their crockers for dozens of years didn't undiscover themselves and now they're there, machines that work whose sibling models in other parts of the world can only be found in museums.
Where to go?
The best-knowing place for most visitors doesn't change.
For this reason, it is not necessary to talk about Havana or Varadero anymore.
Maybe just to remember that in Hemingway's well-quoted hacienda there's a museum that for those who are writer lovers, shouldn't fail to visit.
The Viñales valley is Cuba &apos; stewth's most picturesque corner to the west of the island.
Exuberant vegetation that grows in limestone lands.
Mogotes, limestone pylons and steep tall sprawling rocks in the valley's falls, and their caves acclimatize fertile lands thanks to which prawns that hide dense vegetation are formed.
The valley is probably cultivated the highest-quality tobacco in Cuba.
Visiting some of the habaneras factories of cigars you can find its final product.
Maybe somebody finds it exciting that the Chilean part of the series, Major Zeman, was filmed there.
Trinity
An old-fashion town that was founded not long after the Spaniards colonized the island.
Slavery and Sugar brought fame and wealth.
Wealth became a beauty with buildings in the small town.
Spaniard colonial atectuation, especially influenced by French colonists arriving later in four centuries, has barely changed.
Trinity, between the sea and the Escambray mountains, is such a remote place that even in the 50s of the last century there was no road connecting it with the rest of the country.
Such insulation helped her stay out of the rest.
Everything new was built and for that reason, either, nothing was destroyed, because each person was happy to have where to live.
If there's more time to be in Trinidad than a single afternoon, it's worth knowing interesting places in the nearby surroundings such as Ancón beach or the sugar factory valley.
The evening belongs to the House of Music, which animates with its music the silence of the mornings.
Baracoa
A small city in the far west of Cuba, some regard it as the most beautiful on the island.
Surprisingly, this opinion is already confirmed on the road itself.
The two roads that arrive there from Guantanamo must pass through tall and broken mountains, as well as by a nature that barely knows man's action.
This eastern place of Cuba has been inscribed as a World Heritage Site by UNESCO thanks to its untouched nature.
There they do the best chocolate, in their church they have the Cross of Columbus (in reality it is a cross that arrived 100 years after the disembark) and from the hill, where the castle was located and that now there is a hotel with its same name, you can see the mountains and among them the best known, the Anvil.
Coffee, rum, cigar, the Sierra Maestra, the Bahía de los Cochinos and the island of Pinos, Cabaret Tropicana, the patron saint of Cuba, the Virgen de la Caridad del Cobre, the Santería, an African religion practiced by the descendants of those slaves, many pages could all be filled.
However, as it is said, it's better to see it sometime.
Allies Are Passive in Afghanistan, Americans Say
Few soldiers and insufficient necessary equipment is what is influencing the progress of the mission in Afghanistan.
NATO, according to American Defense Minister Robert Gates, should devise a concrete plan for the next few years.
The mission in Afghanistan isn't stunning as it was thought, the representatives of the countries that have troops in the southern part of the country coincided in Edinburgh (Scotland).
At the same time as conflicts with the Taliban movement are growing, the eight participating states today discuss the plans and co-ordination of the allies.
While the U.S. this week through its defense minister, Robert Gates, encouraged NATO forces for greater activity, Britain sees strengthening the Afghan government &apos; s authority and the distribution of development aid.
"Development in Afghanistan is palpable but the armed forces can only be one of the solutions.
The next one is to make it possible for the people that they alone can start economic and security development," Defense Minister Des Browne told the BBC broadcaster.
Ninety per cent
The activity of the Afghan rebels according to data from the U.S. army is greater than in the last five years.
Violent shares have increased compared to the last year by 27 per cent, and in Helmand province up to 60 per cent.
NATO countries in Afghanistan have about 40,000 troops of whom a quarter are in the south of the territory.
For the U.S., however, the allies have reached the same limit in terms of organizing and equipping the units.
Exactly they call for more helicopters, soldiers and pundits to train the Afghan units.
"We're going to have to think seriously about concrete goals for the future and the way we'll achieve them," Gates said.
NATO Secretary-General Jaap de Hoop also spoke in a similar sense today.
"I'm not glad to know what we've got on earth and sky in Afghanistan.
We've got ninety per cent of soldiers and equipment we'd need, and that's little," De Hoop told Reuters at the close of the three-ha-day meeting with the Japanese government in Tokyo.
Who's passive
Among the participating countries in recent months there has been the controversy as to whether all the allies make the maximum in Afghanistan.
Germany, for example, was criticised, for example.
"The Germans have a relative number of replacements in Afghanistan.
"But they are in an area of minimal violence and the fighting demands there are not as lofty in ours, in the southern and eastern parts of the country," Gates said.
The sensationalist newspaper Sun this week called the German soldiers lazy.
"Afghan Rebellies discover that the Germans dedicate themselves to gambling while our lads are fighting," Sun wrote in material accompanied by photographs of German soldiers playing table tennis.
The German Bild responded to the attack and published a photograph where the British troops played a rugby match in the southern part of Afghanistan.
The debt of the state will reach one billion in 2009
For every Czech state owes 84,000 kroner.
The total debt in September amounted to 842.7 billion kronor.
Earlier this year the government planned to borrow 159.2 billion kronor.
At the beginning of December, however, the ministry reduced the plan to 152.8 billion kronor thanks to an improvement in developments in the state's budget economy.
The State debt of the Czech Republic grew at the end of September to 842.7 billion kronor.
In the nine months of this year it rose by 40.2 billion kronor.
However, since June it has dropped 13.4 billion kronor.
Thus today, the Ministry of Economy confirmed its data on state debt in early October.
For every Czech the debt corresponds to 84,000 kronor.
Across the past year, the state was owed more than 111 million kronor.
"For now it seems that thanks to next year's low deficit we could cross the billion-dollar border until 2009.
Next next year the debt should move between $90bn and $1 trillion," he said for server iHNed.cz, Homeland Finance economist David Marek said.
In the same way he also sees the economist of Next Finance, Vladimír Pikora: "We could spend the billion during 2009.
Exactly when that will depend on the next budget that we don't yet know.
If the situation on the political scene were to be seen to change and the politicians were able to save, then it could be delayed to 2010.
I don't see such a change," he added.
Doubt grows
The debt of the state creates debts in the central government and arises, above all, from the accumulation of the deficit of the state budget.
State debt is paid through treasury bonds, state bonds, direct credits or for example loans from the European Investment Bank.
The state's domestic indebtedness grew this year from 38.5 billion to 71.9 billion kroner.
Outdoor debt so far has grown from 1.7 billion kronor to 123.3 billion kroner.
The so-called negotiable state debt at the end of September contained $80.3 billion kronor since June fell by 13.7 billion kronor.
The average maturity of state debt increased by 6.5 years.
"The total year-on-year debt from the first to the third trimester created 13 per cent which is huge given how the economy works.
It is interesting that the growth is generated above all from obligations where there is 20 per cent growth.
It's bad that the debt grows but on the other hand it shows that it's going to be negotiated until there's a pension reform," Pikora said.
Better than expected
It is extracted from the state's payment and debt management strategy for 2007 that the government planned to borrow 152.2 billion kronor this year.
At the beginning of December, the ministry lowered the plan by 152.8 billion kronor thanks to an improvement in developments in the state budget.
The amount consists, above all, of the deficit of the state budget that this year will be 76 million kroner instead of the planned 91.3 billion kroner.
The rest consists of maximum resources planned for the Czech Consolidation Agency with about 13.3 billion kronor, the reserve of 10 billion kronor for the payment of pensions, 43 billion kronor to finance the state's obligations and 1.8 billion kroner in payments of the European investment bank's loans.
He compared Jarúm today in front of a court to have given a teddy bear a name.
The British teacher tried in Sudan to insult Islam has been sentenced on Thursday afternoon by a Khartoum court to 15 days &apos; p.m. imprisonment and expulsion, her lawyer said.
The British woman was accused of letting pupils ages 6 to 7 give Mahomet's name a teddy bear when any depiction of the prophet of Islam is illegal.
He was subject to a lash penalty.
This 54-year-old British schoolteacher based at Unity High School in Khartoum in Sudan has been arrested on Sunday at her home and charged with attack on Islam and sediction under article 125 of the Sudanese Criminal Code, as a result of a number of complaints from parents of pupils to the Sudanese Ministry of Education.
The story actually dates back to the month of September, when this teacher asks her 6/7-year-old students to choose a name for the teddy bear in the class.
"Eight names come out of the children's mouths, such as Hassan, Abdullah and Mohammed ," says school principal Robert Boulos.
Ultimately, after a vow in class, 20 children out of 23 have chosen Mohamed as their favorite name.
Mohamed is, of course, the Muslim prophet's name, but above all a very common name in Sudan.
