This is, of course, a matter of interpretation.
They did not reach an agreement on the future status of the province on Wednesday, during the last negotiating session to Vienna.
Ultimate in the end, failure.
The Serbs and Albanians of Kosovo have ended on Wednesday morning in Baden in Austria a sixth and final session of discussions without being able to overcome their differences on the future status of the separatist province.
"It is regrettable that the parties have not been able to reach an agreement on the status," said European mediator Wolfgang Ischinger, during a press conference in Vienna.
Underlinlining that peace in the region was "at stake ," his American counterpart, Frank Wisner, urged Belgrade and Pristina to respect their efforts to preserve peace and dialogue beyond the end of the negotiations.
"We don't foresee violence at the moment but are entering a very difficult period," he added, pointing out that "obvious tensions exist ."
End Quote on 10 December
Troika mediators, rejoining the European Union, the United States and Russia, will go one last time on Monday in Serbia and in Kosovo.
The future of the province populated with 90 per cent of Albanians could become clearer when Ischinger, Wisner and Russian mediator Alexander Botsane-Khartchenko will return their relationship to the U.N. Security Council by December 10.
Baden's discussions near Vienna constituted the sixth and final session of negotiations since the Troika has been in charge of further mediation by the United Nations last August.
U.S. and Europeans believe that their mediation will be completed on December 10.
The Russians, who have already blocked in the Security Council the granting of independence in Kosovo, insist on a pursuit of the negotiations beyond this deadline.
"Russia was and is compromised in favour of a negotiated solution, a compromise solution ," Botsane-Khartchenko said.
Unilateral Declaration of Independence?
After eight years of U.N. administration in Kosovo, Westerners consider independence under European Union supervision to be the only viable solution.
Pristina is prepared to proclaim it unilaterally if the Security Council does not resolve on its behalf.
Belgrade is insisting on increased autonomy.
"Unfortunately, no agreement has been found ," announced the president of the Kosovo Autonomous Authority, Fatmir Sejdiu, at the Baden hotel where these "last-opportunity negotiations have been held since Monday."
Hashim Thaci, a former commander of the Kosovo Rebellion and the province's future prime minister, has given his grudges.
But he added that the plan of autonomy presented by Belgrade " belonged to a past that has led to manslaughters and deportations."
" Dominated Effect"
Serbian President Borís Tadic, for his part, considered that a compromise was always feasible, warning the Albanian majority in Kosovo against the consequences of a unilateral proclamation of the independence of the province.
Belgrade particularly claims to fear a "dominant effect" in a region still weakened by the independence wars of the 1990s.
"For Serbia, he said, this doesn't mean that a compromise is impossible."
On Tuesday, the Serbian delegation had insisted that any decision belongs exclusively to the Security Council, in which its Russian ally has a right of veto.
"The Kosovo Parliament will have the last word"
"Only a solution within the Security Council is acceptable for Serbia," said Serbian Prime Minister Vojislav Kostunica.
"These negotiations have lasted two years.
They must run out as they have begun: in the Security Council."
To the journalists, Fatmir Sedjiu had replied that "the Kosovo Parliament will have the last word on the statute ," adding that any unilateral declaration of independence by the Albanians would be made "in consultation" with the Westerns.
Bush kicks off an agreement between Israelis and Palestinians
U.S. President George Bush announced, during the opening of the international peace conference gathered by himself on Tuesday to Annapolis in Maryland that the Israelis and Palestinians had finally reached agreement on a joint statement.
He said Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Olmert and Palestinian President Mahmoud Abbas had reached an immediate agreement in the face of finding a final regulation of the 60-year-old contentious point between their two peoples.
In the presence of both men, Bush vowed that he would do everything to help them complete a "peace treaty" before the end of 2008.
Palestinians, meanwhile, must dismantle "Terrorist infrastructures" and Israel must put an end to its colonization activities, Bush said, reminding both sides of their obligations regarding the itinerary for peace they accepted in 2003.
The head of the White House stressed that the negotiations between Israel and the Palestinian Authority chaired by Abbas would "adventure a peace treaty that would resolve all outstanding issues, without exception, including central issues."
This denomination covers the three main wobbly problems at the Israeli-while point: the future borders of Palestine, the status of Jerusalem and the fate of Arab refugees expelled from their homes during the 1948 conflict.
Taking the floor after the U.S. president, Abbas insisted that the up-to-coming negotiations be "global and demeaning" and refer to in all aspects of a regulation, citing "Jerusalem, refugees, borders, colonies, water, security, among other things ."
Climbing his return to the rostrum, Olmert pledged to what the coming negotiations are "direct and continuous" and to make "an effort to conclude them in the course of 2008.
At the end of the year 2008, Bush set out the end of 2008 the conclusion of his "vision of two Palestinian and Israeli-seas" that co-exist.
