Tension rises in Pakistan
The Pakistani police reprimand on Monday reprimanded the first demonstrations since General Musharraf's state of emergency two days earlier.
In the city of Lahore, in the east of the country, about 2,000 lawyers protesting the measures taken within the framework of the state of emergency have been injured by tear gas grenades used by law enforcement, according to witnesses.
The latter replicated with stone shots, injuring cops.
At least 350 lawyers have been questioned.
Scenes similar to Karachi and Rawalpindi, where cops loaded and wounded lawyers.
Many would have been questioned.
In Islamabad, the Supreme Court's lawyers demanded a general strike and announced demonstrations, following a wave of about 500 arrests over the weekend of opponents, human rights activists and lawyers.
The streets that gave access to the Supreme Court were blocked on Monday in the morning to prevent the grouping.
Other rallies reportedly took place in Multan (centre) and Peshawar (north-northwest).
The lawyers' movement on Monday picked up the breadwinner of revolving religious parties in the powerful Muttahida Majlis-e-Amal alliance whose president "worked the population to send flowers to the judges who spoke out against the state of emergency and the waste they swearing in front of Musharraf ."
" Musharraf's Promise Doesn't Keep More"
The Pakistani Supreme Court is directly referred to the emergency measure proclaimed on Saturday afternoon.
He had to rule in the next few days on the legality of the re-selection to the presidential elections, on October 6, the indirect suffrage of President Musharraf.
The President of the Court, Iftikhar Mohammed Chaudhry, has been removed and replaced.
"Everything we attend is illegal, unconstitutional and contrary to the Supreme Court's rulings," the judge told The News newspaper.
Pervez Musharraf's promise to leave the head of the army and become a civilian president "doesn't value anymore" after the establishment of the state of emergency, Deputy Information Minister Tariq Azeem said on Monday.
He was expected to leave his military uniform by 15 November, at the end of his first presidential term.
The Assistant Information Minister also confirmed that the January elections could be moved.
On the other hand, the Pakistani government denied insistent rumours in the country that President Musharraf would have been assigned to residence by his deputy head of the armies.
