My GP gave me one of his articles on rare research entitled: 'Money produces brain activations, especially if others receive less.'
In other words, the feelings generated by the workers' monthly pay depends on what the comrades earn.
The study has been carried out using tomographic magnetic resonance imaging at the University of Bonn, and the results are published in the last issue of Science.
According to Bernd Weber, the author of the study and director of Life&Brain's Neurocognition Imaging Group in Germany: "The strongest activation took place when the participant responded correctly and his partner failed."
Investigators observed that if they were both successful they received the same payment, the activation was moderate, but if one received twice as much, the activation was higher.
I don't know if this conclusion required so much science.
The prosecutor of the Balkans
Carla del Ponte has relentlessly persecuted the war criminals of the Balkans for eight years from the prosecutor &apos; s Office of the Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia, located in The Hague.
His mandate expires at the end of the year and Carla will leave with a bittersweet flavor, because the main perpetrators of the Srebrenica massacre - in which 8,000 Muslim Bosnians died - former General Ratko Mladic and former Bosnian Serb leader Radovan Karadzic remain at large.
Their names are on that list from which the documentary of the Swiss filmmaker Marcel Schspbach, 'La lista de Carla' (2006, unreleased in Spain) that, with the rhythm of a police thriller, follows the work of the Swiss prosecutor from Belgrade to New York to persecute the fleeing criminals and pressure the governments involved to narrow the fence.
What's enough for me?
Automobile insurance, which covers the driver in the face of possible casualties, is a significant expense that will depend on various factors, such as age, sex, card seniority, marital status, the type of vehicle to be insured or the use thereof, among others.
Having the car is just the beginning.
Choosing the right car insurance is even harder than choosing the vehicle model.
First of all we have to be clear about what we're talking about.
The car insurance is an agreement between an insurance company and the insured company.
This commitment materializes in an insurance contract, which provides that the company receives the value of the agreed premium in exchange for being responsible for the obligation to pay compensation in the event of a sinister.
The premium is the price of insurance, which is established taking into account a number of factors, such as the age, sex, the seniority of the card, marital status, the type of vehicle to be secured or the use of the vehicle, among others.
Insuring companies set their prices according to the statistics, so more likely to suffer an accident, a greater fee to be paid.
That's why those over the ages of 25 and women have to pay lower premiums, as the statistics show they have a lower casualty rate because of their prudence.
Also shaking the counts, the companies have come to the conclusion that married people tend to have fewer accidents than unmarried drivers.
The use of the vehicle will have an impact on the frequency, and thus the probability of having some kind of mishap.
By increasing the frequency the odds increase.
There are many variables that insurers take into account when establishing the premium price.
It is therefore that the driver has to study the situation well in order to choose car insurance that best suits his profile.
We will present here the most significant variables when choosing one or the other type of insurance, so that this, in addition to adapting to the characteristics of the insurer, will offer you a reasonable price.
Young people under the age of 25 are faced with a number of inconvenience when it comes to contracting insurance at an affordable price, such as the reduced experience in driving and the seniority of the card.
Although the ideal to be well-insured is full-than-risk insurance, there are other cheaper options.
The most cautious drivers can opt for a risk with franchise, so that they get the coverage of insurance at full risk but at a more feasible price.
This type of agreement would involve the customer paying a share of the amount of the accident.
"The share of the client's sinister will be pre-established according to the value of the premium, which will be the higher the lower the amount," says Luis Noguera, head of Spain of First Europa, online insurance mediator.
Another option is an extended third-party insurance, so that the customer ensures protection against damage caused to third-party, although not from those caused in his or her own car.
In addition, you'd be covered by burglary, arson and the loss of the vehicle.
A piece of advice.
If you are under the age of 25 you try to be careful and avoid casualties as much as possible, as this will have an impact on the bonus, being able to reduce the cost of insurance in the future.
A bonus is a reduction over the company's base rate.
On the contrary, a penalty is a surcharge on that base rate.
"The bonuses and penalties will depend on the company and the benefit of the company.
"If the company has had a good year you will be able to afford to increase the bonuses," says Noguera.
After the 25-year swath, drivers have the eager to make it easier when it comes to hiring insurance, since the casualty rate is reduced thanks to the accumulated experience.
For this reason, it's most advisable to hire a risk with franchise.
"Unoriginally young people cannot make use of the experience previously acquired when it comes to using an accident, and even when it comes to driving," says the director in Spain of the European company, adding that most accidents occur in the second year of driving, after the 'fear of the rookie' has passed.
However, if the client is married but does not yet have any children it is preferable for him to negotiate insurance at full risk, as long as his economic situation permits, of course.
For their part, marriages with children are regarded by insurers as the public with the lowest casualty rate, so the surcharges will be lower.
In this case the best insurance would be one at full risk (possessing that of 300 euros), or, when there is a high level of rendition available, one to third parties expanded from the third year of the life of the vehicle.
"They're going through more relaxed driving and they have a greater purchasing power to afford to take on a larger franchise amount," says Luis Noguera.
For retireees it is advisable for full-than-responsive-the-responsive-the-usfrancget insurance (several of 150 euros), or one extended to third parties when you have a high level of rendition.
It has to be said that automotive insurance is an important thing, not only because it covers the driver in the face of possible accidents, but also because it represents an important expense, which is why it is essential to properly study the insurance that suits us best according to our personal situation.
The Celtic are searching Milan for a point worth the eighth
Milan and Celtic are measured this Tuesday in San Siro in search of the point they lack to achieve their respective objectives in the closing of the 'Champions' liguilla, as the Italians serve to be first and the Scots to sentence their classification.
The Italians add up to ten points and they have already certified in the fifth day his pass with a draw in Portugal, while Celtic has nine points, but he still needs another one to make sure that Shakhtar, with six, and that he receives Benfica, who has four, does not put him forward.
These two Group D matches will be played a week before the end of the entire sixth day, as Milan has to play the Club World Cup in Japan, where it debuts on the 13th in semifinals in search of a place for the final of 16.
Thus, this group will be closed before the rest and the tie in the Giuseppe Meazza seems the most anticipated result, although the two teams will try to go out for a victory, as the Italians won't want to speculate at home and Celtic know that winning would stay first and ensure a better crossing in eighths.
Gordon Strachan's ensemble could even serve a defeat to qualify if the Shakhtar does not win, but he will not be able to be confidited because at a nine-point pass the pass is for the Ukrainians, who lost last week in Glasgow (2-1), but won (2-0) in the other direct duel.
Thus, the Milanists will try to sour the night to the Scots, who are trusted to get in eighths for the second time in the restructured Champions League and in a consecutive way, after they were precisely defeated in that round last campaign with a Kaka goal in the extension (1-0) after 0-0 of the one-way.
Waiting for the 'rossoneri' victory there will be the Shakhtar who is only worth the triumph, in case he loses the Celtic, both to get in the eighth and to stay in UEFA.
Benfica, despite being the last and with no options to remain in the maximum European competition, could reach third place with a victory, so UEFA would play.
20,000 people attend Spice Girls to tour gorge in Vancouver
The Spice Girls have started in Vancouver, Canada, in front of about 20,000 spectators, its first tour since they dissolved in 2000.
"Posh Spice" (Victoria Beckham), 'Scary Spice' (Melanie Brown), 'Baby Spice' (Emma Bunton), 'Sporty Spice' (Melanie Chisholm) and 'Ginger Spice' (Geri Halliwell) testified shortly before starting their concert at Vancouver's GM Place Stadium (Emma Bunton).
Around 20,000 people sold out the tickets to witness the concert that included a video recounting the history of the group that made furore at the end of the 1990s.
The reunion and the world tour coincide with the release of a new album that collects its main hits as well as two new songs, 'Headlines' and 'Voodoo', which in Spain publishes EMI.
The five members of Spice Girls came out on stage wearing bracelets given to 'Posh Spice', British footballer David Beckham.
The athlete was members of the public who crowded the GM Place, even though Victoria's woes had banned him days earlier.
In the lead-up to the show, Victoria Beckham pointed out that the British star "hasn't seen our wardrobe at all, so I'm excited about what she's going to think of the show."
"Sporty Spice" whose departure of the group in 1998 "because of the differences between us" caused the dissolution of the quintet two years later, he also expressed his nervousness during a meeting with the press, two hours before the start of the show.
"She was scared.
I've been nervous but I've seen the feeling of the world have changed, the feeling for the Spice Girls has changed.
And I didn't think why not, so I'm glad I've made the decision," Chisholm said of the meeting of the group she dissolved nine years ago.
The singer also pointed out that over time, "Things have been fixed and we all love each other very much."
But a lot of things have changed since the Spice Girls last performed together in 1998.
To begin with, according to CTV television, the group arrived in Vancouver on a special Virgin Airlines plane.
Upon their arrival at the airport, an army of nannies awaited them.
Among the five there are seven children.
After the Vancouver concert, the group will perform in California (USA), in San Jose (December 4) and Los Angeles (5 and 7 December).
In Madrid they will perform on December 23.
New Fee Fears on Gas Between Russia and Ukraine
The growing dispute over the price of gas between Ukraine and Russia is increasing the risk that it will affect the export of fuel to Western Europe by Russia.
The majority of gas exported by Russia to the European Union (EU) is directed through Ukraine, so every friction between the two nations is a source of attention.
Kiev has warned that if Moscow increases the price of gas, Russia will have to pay higher transit rates.
An earlier dispute last year reduced supplies to EU states.
The last dispute between Kiev and Moscow came with the Ukrainian government and the Russian state, monopoly gas owner Gazprom, attempting to agree on the amount of money Ukraine was supposed to pay for Russian gas in 2008.
Gazprom stated that it will charge Ukraine more, as it has pledged to pay an extra 50 per cent in 2008 for the gas that comes from neighbouring Turkmenistan.
Analysts say that Ukraine could pay 40 per cent more for Gazprom's imports.
"It is important for us to use our arguments as one of the most important transits for gas during the talks," Ukrainian Economy Minister Anatoly Kinakh told the Reuters news agency.
"Changes in gas prices shouldn't be related to our arguments about the levels of transit rates [to Western Europe] via Ukraine."
The last major gas dispute between Moscow and Kiev Gazprom severed supplies to Ukraine in January 2006.
This marked a fall in exports to Western Europe, which quickly pushed up prices.
Russian gas accounts for one-hundred-than-eout of the EU.
Russia has the world's largest gas reserves, but much of it isn't yet fully developed.
As a result, Gazprom complements its own gas supply from Turkmenistan and other former Soviet Central Asian nations.
NHS "should test foreign workers"
The Scottish NHS needs to control the criminal records of employees from outside Europe, a watchdog has said.
Auditor-General Robert Black's recommendation came after it was found that a junior GP was linked to the Glasgow airport attack in June.
Black has also expressed concern that the Scottish NHS has no accurate idea of the number of foreign staff she has.
However, his report pointed to other controls that are carried out correctly through a sample of five health authorities.
The auditor-in-the-n-general checked the number of foreign staff hired outside the European economic area in the wake of Glasgow's airport attacks.
Bilal Abdullah, 28, an Iraqi doctor at the Royal Alexandra Hospital in Paisley, was arrested at the airport after colliding a jeep at the gates of the airport's terminal building.
He was one of the three doctors who performed at the Old Bailey by video conference in October, charged in connection with the alleged car bomb attacks in the United Kingdom.
The NHS expects to carry out pre-job checks for candidates, to make sure they meet the qualifications, experience and permission to work in the UK.
Although Scotland's health board uses "Disclosure Scotland" to carry out criminal background checks, the agency has no jurisdiction to obtain this information outside of Britain.
The auditor-general's report advises, among other measures, that the boards should be guided by the Criminal Records Bureau, which helps employers with criminal controls in many countries outside of Britain.
"Although we found some of the personal records with evidence of these controls, this is not a routine review," the report says.
Mr Black said the Scottish NHS had a high level of compliance with its own employment policies, but said: "Our report highlights the areas that can be upgraded to ensure that the necessary information is available on the number of NHS foreign staff."
BMA Scotland chairman Dr Peter Terry said it was "very uncommended" pre-employment checks could identify potential NHS employees who could commit terrorist acts in the future.
"The report suggests pre-job checks work on NHS Scotland but it's not clear the added value of the recommendations made," he said.
The Scottish health authorities say they currently have 1,161 foreigners, most of them doctors and nurses
But the auditor-general warned that the number may be higher, in part because the boards have trouble identifying the employees who have indefinite residency permits.
The inquest also found that verification procedures established by NHS authorities in Ayrshire and Arran, Grampian, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Lanarkshire and Lothian were being fully implemented.
This includes passport checks, a work permit and qualification check.
The Scottish government is currently overhauling the pre-job policy and guidance for foreigners in the NHS.
Health Secretary Nicola Sturgeon said: "It was of great interest in carrying out this work in the wake of the Glasgow airport terror attack.
He added that the Scottish government, in conjunction with the health boards, could consider the recommendations of the reports and that this will be reflected in updating the pre-job control guidelines for all working groups - including foreigners - which they planned to publish next month.
Joy Division dominates film awards
The biographical film about Joy's Late Worm vocalist, Ian Curtis, has won five awards at the British Independent Film Festival, including the award for the best film.
The trophies were also given to the protagonist Sam Riley, who worked in a warehouse until he was picked for the role of Curtis, director Anton Corbijn and co-star Toby Kebbell.
Actress Judi Dench was named Best Actress for the thriller "Notes of a Scandal."
Bond star Daniel Craig has won the Variety Award for putting a British film on the international stage.
The awards were presented at a ceremony at the Roundhouse in London.
Control follows Curtis' rise to faculty faculty with Joy Division until his suicide in 1980, at the age of 23.
Footage - in black-and-white - featuring The Killers, David Bowie and New Order soundtrack - was produced at just 3,000.
Dutch photographer Corbijn, already known for the iconic postcard of U2's iconic album Joshua Tree, was also awarded two awards - for best director and best debuting director.
