Saudi Arabia Reaches 208 Terrorists
Saudi Arabia revealed yesterday that it has arrested 208 terrorist militants planning an impending attack on oil facilities, as well as attacks on religious and security forces.
State-fistorium television of the world's largest oil supplier said that one of the cells was planning to smuggle missiles.
Sympathizers of Al-Qaeda have mounted a campaign against the local monarchy, in alliance with the United States, since 2003 following the invasion of Iraq.
An eight-strong cell formed by foreign residents was planning an attack on an oil facility in the eastern province, the television report said.
Saudi Arabia created a 35,000-strong force to protect the facilities of Abqaiq, the world's largest oil plant, following a failed Al Qaeda attack in 2006.
The report, citing a statement by the Saudi Interior Minister, said 18 of those arrested belong to a cell led by a "missile-threw expert," who has entered the country.
The ministry said the militants had planned smuggling eight projectiles into the country.
A further 22 of those arrested were planning killings of religious leaders and security forces.
The U.S. government warned religious leaders that they should be more active in stopping the Saudis traveling to Iraq to unite as al-Qaeda militants to fight Allied forces and to support the Muslim Shia government, which is seen as heretic by the most extreme Saudi Suns.
Al Qaeda's militants are targeted by many religious in Saudi Arabia who have been appointed by the authorities to support the policies of the government-dominating royal family.
The militants - sweeped by calls from Osama Bin Laden, the Saudi-born Al Qaeda leader to harass the country &apos; s pro-Western government - were targeting residences of government-associated foreigners, government buildings and energetic facilities.
"This was a great effort by the security forces for five months of work," the interior minister's spokeswoman told state television.
Those arrested also include a "media cell" of 16 people in Medina, whose objective was to promote "takfiri thinking" - the ideology of radical Sunni Muslims who support violence against Muslims considered infidel and apostates.
The arrest includes 32 other people accused of providing support and financial support to the militants.
Clime of Venus can give an idea of the future of the Earth
"Venus may have once been a suitable place for life," scientists said yesterday.
Data from a European spacecraft in orbit showed the planet most similar to an Earth-like twin than had previously been observed.
Venus is the closest planet to Earth.
The two planets are similar in size and occupy similar orbits, although Venus is slightly closer to the sun.
Millions of years ago, Venus may have had conditions similar to those that made the Earth a habitable planet, scientists now believe.
But the effects of carbon dioxide and the greenhouse effect, the atmospheric erosion by solar radiation and the slower solar rotation sent Venus to a very different type of development.
Today the planet doesn't have any water, the temperature of the surface is sufficient to melt lead and is eroded by hurricane winds and lightning storms.
The results of the European Space Agency for the Venus Express, which has studied the planet for a year, provide an overview of how the Earth could develop catastrophic changes because of the climate.
Professor Fred Taylor, from Oxford University's physics and one of the leaders of the Venus Express mission, said: "It's becoming clear why the weather on Venus is so different from that of the Earth, when it's two planets so similar.
"Our new data makes it possible to build a scenario in which Venus began as the Earth, possibly with a habitable environment millions of years ago."
