Mitt Romney says it's not worthy for presidential nominees to answer snow monkey's questions.
However, Republican presidential nominees eventually agree to hold a debate on YouTube, in which ordinary voters shoot up video questions and send them for dissemination among the candidates.
Neither of the questions presented on the afternoon of Wednesday, November 28, were more ridiculous than the snow monkey who asked Democrats about global warming in the YouTube Republican party debate.
But the questions, and the answers, this time reflected a lot from the state of the Republican party ahead of next year's elections.
It's not known exactly how CNN's producers, who co-sponsored the debate, chose the YouTube videos to show the nominees for aspiring candidates.
But the first half-hour of debate, the longest spent on a single issue, focused on immigration.
The issue is one of the most powerful topics for Republicans.
CNN asked an undecided group of voters to raise the dial when they heard ideas they would like: emitters noticed sprinkles of congressional approval when the candidates spoke with extremist arguments.
The toughest-doing exchanges of the night occurred between Rudy Giuliani, the national front-runner, and Mr. Romney, who has been a leader in the crucial Iowa and New Hampshire caucuses.
Mr. Giuliani is trying to wreck Romney's reputation because he was soft on illegal immigration when he was mayor of New York.
He shootered Mr. Romney, accusing the former Massachusetts governor of hiring illegal immigrants to work at his home.
Both responded aggressively when they were asked if a candidate could support an "amnesty" (allowing the illegals to regularize their situation and giving them the opportunity to obtain citizenship).
Minor nominees such as Duncan Hunter, a California congresswoman, and Tom Tancredo, a Colorado congressman, reached agreement on the issue.
Neither has the word to be nominee, but they both remain in the race perhaps because they both warned about the issue of immigration rather than the others.
At least on immigration, and broadly speaking, the candidates share their views: increased surveillance at the borders, intolerance towards illegal immigrants.
This topic, coupled with the fear of China's toxic toys and other worries about abroad, helps Republicans to forget, or minimize, the divisions between liberals and Christian conservatives.
But the debate became uncomfortable when other issues appeared.
Mr. Giuliani was asked why he had supported gun control in the past.
John McCain and Mr. Romney debated whether diving in water should be considered torture.
One person asked whether the candidates believed in every Bible word.
This led Mr. Giuliani to stumbling towards the position that although the Bible was his favorite book, he didn't literally believe in the history of Jonah and whale.
Another person asked whether Jesus would support the death penalty.
Mike Huckabee (ESC) - now, against all expectations, first in the most recent Iowa poll, thanks to his long-running Christian conservatism - said the death penalty was painful but necessary.
When he was pressured to answer whether Jesus would be in favor, he laughed, saying that Jesus was too smart to be a candidate in the elections.
Race remains unusually open.
Mr. Huckabee in Iowa, coincidentally with Liberal Ron Paul's fundraising campaigns, and their heated statements from Wednesday's debate.
They've come out from the bottom, 'because the two wings of the party didn't agree on the most famous and traditional candidate.
In 1980 and 2000, a candidate united the party early and convincingly with a winning message about the greatness of America, satisfying both wing wings of the party.
This time the party is divided and the result is far from clear.
Labour screams
Gordon Brown has unveiled another hole to sink in, but it also emerges
As the political scandals continue in England, this is not particularly interesting.
They don't seem to have sold influences, nor are these exorbitant hotel beads in Paris, or sucked with extramarital fingers.
But it has been revealed that a wealthy businessman has disguised contributions to Britain's Labour governing party of more than $650,000 ($1.3 million), for about four years using fake names, and that the party did not denounce it.
Two months ago, when the Prime Minister evaded an election, stupidly insulting British intelligence by stating that opinion polls did not influence his decision, the hope was that it would provide a strong and efficient government.
But instead, like Vince Calbe's Vince Calbe, the Liberal Democrat leads with increasing ingenuity because the dreaded Mr. Brown has gone from Stalin to being Mr Bean.
The resurgence of Northern Rock, a troubled bench, demonstrated the regulatory weakness and poverty of judgment in times of crisis.
Bad proposals to change the tax code has put business groups, together, against the government.
The fiscal authority lost 25 million personal data that revealed an unimaginable administrative slope.
A trio of retiring generals started a mutiny in the House of Lords, protesting against cuts to military spending: being ministers of the armed forces is, they claimed, a part-time job.
It's now the multiple donations from David Abrahams; Peter Watt, the Labour secretary-general (and former complicit) has already resigned.
None of this means that Mr. Brown moves irrevocably towards the exit.
Governments can reach a tipping point after they find themselves unable to govern.
People disbelieve them as politicians, but they usually suspend their disbelief when a new batch takes over.
Once it is clear that the Prime Minister cannot improve things, that he doesn't have much time left, that suspension is too long: public officials are beginning to fail; cabinet ministers are exposed; the press is looking for bad news; and the government gets on the defensive and doesn't focus.
On the one hand, Brown's position is particularly tough, because unlike other political leaders he can't defend himself by arguing that he's new.
Even if he wanted to deny Tony Blair, he was his senior lieutenant.
The Northern Rock problems and the authority registration tax are related to the decisions that Brown made as chancellor of the treasury.
And even if he isn't directly embroiled in Blairish's setbacks - or "partner-wheel-maker," the inquiry into the funding of political parties - voters may assume that if he is.
However, Mr. Brown's situation isn't as bad as it seems.
Irons aren't as bad as the Tories want to show.
As with Mr. Bean, no one has died, nor has he been swindled directly.
And the remediation, while painful, is to give what Mr. Brown's naturally Stalinist tendencies have always promised: a reliable and brutal government.
The new political donation scandal is a good example.
Mr. Abrahams, who compares his donations to charity gifts, says he doesn't want public acknowledgment for his benevolence.
That may be the case, but it's not the decision.
The anti-anonymity laws for big political taxpayers are clear and there's for a clear reason: the public has the right to know who's buying access to those who have the power.
Mr. Watt, who said he knew about Mr. Abraham's agreement, and that it wasn't illegal, will obviously face Jon Mendlesohn, the fundraising chief who was asked about the donations in October.
But Mr. Brown so far isn't implicated: there's nothing to suggest that he knew about these performances, although the bulk of the payments were made after he took control of the office in June.
So far the Prime Minister has done half of the work.
He has been quick to condemn his party for violating the law, but is diluted in decision-making for a family matter, a commission of inquiry into the matter - driven, to a large extent, by a judge and a bishop.
In this case, morals are uncomplicated.
If the law has been breached, it's a police matter.
In addition, there are other things that Mr. Brown must do, such as finding a permanent and independent boss for the Rules Commission in Public Life, because he hasn't had one for six months, and he must also push for a genuine reform of party funding.
This will require hard-to-recool Labour with the Tories, and perhaps relinquishing the special financial situation with trade unions - gorgeous measures for a partisan like Mr. Brown.
But he must deal with some partisan facts.
The Conservatives now have a good level of support in the polls; he'll have two years to win voters; and the only way he can move to victory is to perform with impeccable probity and strong competition.
Shark cards
Is the subprime Mortgage Custom Crisis Infecting the U.S. Credit Card Market?
When there's any blood in the water, it's natural for some dorsal fins to swim thrill around.
Today that the American housing market is in crisis, predators have their eyes on the country's credit card market.
Analysts at Goldman's Sachs estimate that credit card losses can reach $99 million if the subprime mortgage crisis spreads other forms of consumer credit.
Signs of contagion are already seen.
There's an increase in both card charges and crime rates, which measures the proportion of balances that haven't been amortized or are more than 30 days late respectively.
HSBC announced last month that it has taken $1.4 million in its US consumer finance business, in part due to weak card borrowers.
But it's too early to panic.
Lateness and crime are at low levels.
According to Moody's, a ratings agency, in the third trimester the crime rate of 3.89 per cent was almost a percentage point lower than the historical average.
Disenuation of rates can be explained in part by technical factors.
A change in the U.S. bankruptcy law (2005) led to a sharp fall in bankruptcy applications, which in turn represent a large loss on credit cards; the number of applications (and therefore late payments) would be growing again, or conditions for borrowers may have worsened.
The industry also reports solid payment rates, which show how much of its debts consumers pay each month.
And reliance on credit cards and asset-backed securities is pretty strong, despite paralysis in other financial areas.
Dennis Moroney of TowerGroup, a research firm, predicts that emission volumes for 2007 will be about 25 per cent higher than last year.
The direct infection channels of the sub-prime-mortgage-to-mortgment crisis certainly exist: Consumers like to carry credit card debts now that credit institutions are expiring.
But credit card issuers are more looking at the flow of liquidity than active values, so falling prices don't necessarily trigger a change in borrowers' creditor capacity.
They can even work to overtake the issuers.
Inconvenience for consumers to continue amortizing the mortgage decreases if the properties are worth less than the value of the loan; and as a as a result, the payment of the card's debt rises on the priority list.
Card issuers can also respond quickly and flexibly to the difficult conditions for mortgage lenders, changing interest rates or altering credit balances.
That in theory should reduce the risk of a rapid asset reshuffle.
"We won't wake up one day and reassess all the loans again," says Gary Perlin, Capital One's chief financial officer.
If a sudden sub-prime infection in the credit card market is unlikely, the risks of a sustained decline are much more real.
If house prices are lower and a shrink of credit pushing America into recession, the industry undoubtedly faces a more severing future.
You have to keep an eye on those dorsal fins.
Brisbane banners found guilty in Enron case
Three British bankers who went on trial because of their responsibility for the braces with Enron's former boss, Andrew Fastow's financial office, were found guilty on Wednesday.
David Bermingham, Giles Darby and Gary Mulgrew were originally found not guilty of seven counts of bragging for colluding with Fastow in a co-secret financial operation in 2000 to enrich themselves at the expense of employees.
They were sent to trial in January.
But during a hearing before U.S. District Judge Ewing Werlein Jr., each was convicted of collusion in fraud.
The three former Greenwich Natwest chief executives, a unit of the Scottish PLC group at the Royal Bank, became glimpsed in Britain because of extradition proceedings that lasted two years.
In the U.S., his case marks the loose end of an investigation launched after Enron collapse in 2001.
Initially, the charges against the bankers came from having alerted Fastow in 2002 that it was the target of a government investigation into the fall of Enron.
Charamination against the bankers - fierce "NatWest Tres" - alleges that they came to Houston in 2000 to fabricate a fraudulent scheme with Fastow and his previous senior aide, Michael Kopper.
Greenwich Natwest had reinvested in a subsidiary of an Enron company controlled by Fastow, who was the creator of a myriad fraud in Enron that helped his spiral fall into bankruptcy.
In 2002, the bank rated the subsidiary &apos; s interests at zero, but the three Britons knew it would value rising.
A company controlled by Kopper bought the subsidiary's bank interest at $US1 million.
Bankers paid Kopper $250,000 in interest on the company.
According to the accusation, Fastow falsely portrayed Enron because the energy company would pay $20 million to Greenwich NatWest for its share of the subsidiary.
But the $20 million was left in the hands of British bankers, Fastow and others.
Bankers collected $7.3 million, while Fastow, Kopper and others received about $12.3 million, according to the indictment.
In January 2004, Fastow was found guilty on two counts of conspiring in Enron.
The trio of Britons were arrested three months later.
Fastow is serving a six-year sentence at a Louisiana federal prison.
Kopper is serving a three-and-a-month-long sentence in Texarkana.
Each of Britain's three bankers must repay $1 million they required to live in the United States while the trial took place.
Enron, one of the nation's seven-largest companies, declared bankrupt in December 2001 after years of trickery in accounting, couldn't trudge the debt out of millions or make its failing businesses look successful.
Neighborhood left thousands of jobs, more than $US60 million in market terms and more than $2 million in pension schemes.
Enron founder Kenneth Lay and previous chief executive, Jeffrey Skilling, were commissioned last year for their roles in the company's collapse.
Skilling is serving a sentence of more than 24 years.
Lay's conviction for conspiracy, fraud and other charges was adjourned when he died of a heart attack last year.
