It is embarrassing."
The Federal Reserve is expected to slash the rates to lift the stock exchange
Shares leapt on both sides of the Atlantic Yesterday after the market waited for the Fed's reserve to ease credit terms with interest rate cuts, which rose with reassuring comments from US central bank's vice-president.
The stock exchanges loosened in London, New York and through Europe after Donald Kohn, number two Ben Bernanke, the reserve's chairman, declared that policy makers must be "flexible and pragmatic" in responding to the continued tension of financial markets.
In a clear effort to reassure the markets amid growing signs of credit exchanges, Mr Kohn said the recent turbulence was "in part reversed due to the improvement in the market at the end of September and during October."
"If the upward trend persisted, it would increase the possibility of additional adjustments to the financial conditions for real estate and businesses, Kohn said.
The comments were taken by investors as a sign that the reserve is poised to inject fresh capital into U.S. markets and to make subsequent interest rate cuts to stabilize credit conditions.
The comments also helped soothe Wall Street amid bad news for the U.S. economy, such as home sales that reached the lowest record in October.
The consequences of the financial turbulence were accentuated by a notice from Bear Stearns, an investment bank that will slash 650 jobs, which equates to 4 per cent of its total labor, in an overall reduction in costs to offset losses from its U.S. sub-prime mortgages.
Only 20 of those positions are expected to enter London, where the bank employs 1,500 people.
In New York, the Dow Jones industrial average rose 331 points, or 2.55 per cent, to close at 13,289.40, while the S&P 500 index rose 2.65 per cent.
The FTSE 100 index climbed 165.5 points, or 2.7 per cent, to close at 6,306.2, adding more than $39bn to the value of Britain's top-tier companies.
Germany's Dax index was up 2.6 per cent, with France's CAC40 above 2.3 per cent.
Earlier, the reappearance of credit tension in the markets was highlighted after a strong demand at a three-month auction of funds from the European Central Bank, which considered these loans to European commercial banks at 4.7%, the highest rate since April 2001.
Pressure from the Bank of England to follow the actions of the Federal Reserve and the ECB and to inject extra funds into the London stock markets severed credit and three-month "Libor" lending rates among British banks meant the biggest increase since September, at 6.59 per cent, following a steady hike since November 12.
England seize Owais Shah and Ravi Bopara lawsuit
There are two dilemmas here, each as difficult as the other.
Who should beat No 6 for England in Kandy this weekend, Owais Shah or Ravi Bopara?
And, none of them are less important against the first Test against Sri Lanka, who should make the decision?
The second question is relevant because David Graveney, the president of the selection who's on loan - with that particular title at least he is here to help Peter Moores, the coach, and Michael Vaughan, the captain, to decide; and also because the desk of Hugh Morris, the England manager, is beginning to fill with offers to become national selectors, and if not, in one part time.
Whoever doesn't get a sixth-wicket spot will be unlucky, but life is often unfair and the sport too.
It isn't easy to dump what a man does at the top of his profession, while another never goes beyond the lower level, sometimes for lack of luck rather than capacity.
It's not just in cricket the choice that's tough, as judgments are required that always end up being subjective.
It's the right time for Bopara to get up?
It's fair to Shah if he does it?
The same question could be asked in another respect if, says John Edwards or "Tubby" Banerjee, two brilliant young executives with good degrees and who know the business well, had been promoted to the board: there's only room for one of them.
Who needs to say what's a nice level for a cricket player, unless he's given an opportunity to sink or swim to the next chance?
Figures naturally have a great influence on decisions, but so they also have opportunities.
Within a team, a cricket player has to capture the captain's eye at the same time to take a wicket and a batsman to get opportunities to change the order if he wants to have an impact as a player one day.
But catching the eye of the selectors is more complicated.
There were plenty that improved last season, for example, James Anderson, who will surely play in the first test even if Stephen Harmison declares himself to fit and bowls as the wind in the nets.
Anderson took 32 first-class wickets in 33 runs each to earn a place.
Chris Tremlett took just 29 at the same average, but they've both had their high moments and they can expect more from them.
Shah or Bopara games at Asgiriya Stadium can only be a matter of opinion.
