Buses Bring the Refugees Back to Work - and the Fight Against Al Qaeda
The first buses with Iraqi refugees from Syria began to arrive in Baghdad yesterday, expelling the exiles who ran out of cash, without extensions of the visa and those who were waiting for the city to return to normal.
Some had even returned to fight the militants who drove them out of their homes.
At Mansour's bus station, even before the arrival of 800 people from Damascus in an official government convoy consisting of a dozen buses, the terminal was filled with refugees from Syria and Jordan who had returned by private transport.
"I decided to go to Syria after a lot of doctors were killed in Baghdad," says Sabah al-Qubaisi, a Sunni doctor.
"I came back today from what I saw on TV and what I've read in the newspapers, about what happened in Baghdad and it's safe now.'
"They say Mahdi's army stopped killing people," he says, referring to the Shiite militia of religious militant Cleric Moqtada al-Sadr, who ordered his troops a momentary cessation.
Qassim al Atwani, a 38-year-old Shi'ite and teacher in the Sunni area of Amariyah, returned with his two children because it was impossible to renew his visa in Syria because the regime doesn't want the Iraqis working in his country.
His Shiite neighbors in Amariyah called him on the phone to tell him that an allied U.S. force helped drive al-Qaeda out of its stronghold west of the capital, urging him to return.
One of Jordan's newcomers was the head of a Sunni and Shi'ite tribe that had been driven from his home in Sleikh north of Baghdad for a pomegranate attack, after he urged citizens to rise up against al-Qaeda.
He'd come to fight.
"Some days ago, some of my friends called me and they told me they had started an anti-Qaeda militia in Sleikh and that they needed my support and that of my comrades."
"That's why I'm back today," said Sabaah Alu Obeidi, 62.
Many said they have been attracted to the growing calm in the capital since U.S. troops entrenched groups of "concerned citizens," armed and working in cooperation with the U.S. military to defend their neighborhoods.
But many claimed to have returned because of the denial of visas and a lack of money in Damascus, where more than 1.3 million Iraqis settled in recent years.
Salaam al-Samarrai, a 51-year-old father-of-three, voiced the common suspicion among the exiles that Nouri al-Maliki, the Shia prime minister, called on the Syrian government to impose restrictions on refugee visas to force people to return.
More than two and a half million people believe they have fled the country because of the sectarian gangs of death.
"We were okay until al-Maliki visited Syria and urged the government to stop granting visas to the Iraqis, to force them back to Iraq," the Sunni said.
"I can't believe in a government that supports the criminals who set my house on fire after I left it."
Part of the reason for the cessation of the violence is that the Sunnis have been expelled from the Shi'ite areas and vice versa, relaxing the tension.
There are fears that a sudden wave of refugees will reignite sectarian tensions and start the fighting.
The little shoarder, good education.
The smallest federal state is first in vocational training and even shines for the international orientation of its higher schools.
As across the country, the Slanders lacked only an educational field.
"Yesterday I exclaimed a non-wielding non-wieldy that we're not French" - that was the ironic bike about themselves in the smallest federal state for its 50th anniversary.
At the same time France and Luxembourg play a major role in the Sarre education system, which in a study of the Institute of German Economics (IW) called "Bildungsmonitor" ranked fifth.
In this way, higher schools traditionally have good relations with neighbouring countries.
The University of Saarbracken was melted in 1948 by the French.
To this day students can finish their university career here with two or three diplomas.
As a result, the percentage of foreign students is high, at 15, 6 per hundreds.
However, the number of learners from Sarre is higher than an average in higher schools, many of whom do not have a bacillerate.
"The Sartore takes advantage of the system of vocational education," says Axel Plennecke, the education sector expert at the Institute of German Economics (IW).
More than every five young people do their baccalaureate at the Professional School. Thus, it turns out that Sarre is 7 points per cent above the national average.
On the contrary, the range of possibilities for a professional-academic education is very wide.
Offering double racing, in relation to the number of the population, is the second-best in Germany.
Despite practical vocational training and growing demand, the number of graduates in engineering science has fallen by one third in recent years.
That's the reason why the Ministry of Sadland's Ministry of Education forced higher schools to do more publicity for engineering specialties.
"After the first PISA study, we carried out a number of reforms in general education schools," says a spokeswoman for the Ministry of Education.
Thus the government introduced comparative tests across the country.
For the children to start studying the final year of the "Kita-Jahr" project as soon as possible, it has no cost. Meanwhile, this model also mimics the other federal states.
The comparison with the upcoming studies will show whether the performance of students has increased.
According to the 2003 PISA report, the number of students in school development decreased from 29 per cent to 20 per cent.
But, in general, Saar didn't get much better like other federal states.
In Bavaria, the students know how to calculate better.
Unfazed by Salandes, the PISA report does not compare the knowledge of foreign languages, although in this field they could achieve good results.
Although the Sarlandes are not French, the language of the neighbors is a compulsory subject at the Institutes of Secondary Education.
Chavez changes the time.
Soon in Venezuela there will be another different time slider from the rest of the world.
On December 9, President Hugo Chavez lets change the schedule in the South American country by delaying the clock for half an hour.
Chavez is currently staying as a comedian in Spain.
As reported by the Official Gazette of the Venezuelan State, according to the new law, the time will be at less than four hours GMT.
In this way the South American country will have a time zone that does not exist in any other country in the world.
Among other things Chavez, as an enemy of the U.S., justifies his decision by saying that the United States should impose an obligation on the world once by changing a full hour.
In addition, on Wednesday (local time) Chavez announced the rupture of relations with his neighboring country Colombia due to hostage-taking action.
The left-wing nationalist said he wouldn't maintain any relationship with either the head of state or with the Colombian government until the conservative Álvaro Uribe remained the president of Colombia.
"My dignity doesn't allow me to do it," says Chavez, who is pushing for a constitutional reform where the citizens of the oil country must decide Sunday in a referendum.
With this reform you could greatly expand its power in the country.
The reason for the conflict was Uribe &apos; s decision in removing Chavez &apos; s mandate in the negotiations with the Marxist guerrillas dealing with the release of the hostages.
On Sunday Chavez said criticizing that Uribe's decision has offended Venezuela.
There's also a lot of talk in Spain about Chavez.
A new tone of so-called causes sensation in the country: "Why don't you shut up?" is the phrase that half a million people have already unloaded.
This phrase was uttered by the King of Spain who was annoyed by the continuous interruptions on the part of the populists of the left at the last Ibero-American Summit.
In 2007, the decline in the real wage threatens the Germans.
According to a prowess, a decrease in real wages this year threatens German employees despite a strong conjuncture.
The experts present a lot of plenty of clues for pessimistic prognosis.
According to the calculations of the Hans Bockler Foundation, German employees should have in 2007 a lower real living account than the previous year.
"The fault of that is the weak evolution of wages, the increase in value-added tax and the styling of food and energy prices," "Bild" Gustav Horn of the Institute of Macroeconomics and Research of Economic Cycling reported.
New unemployment insurance burdens may also fall to employees.
The Minister of Finance Peter Setinbrack told the newspaper "Neue Ostanbracker Zeitung" that given the current situation, the intention to reduce the unemployment insurance burden by 3.3% from one year to the next is fair and reasonable.
Naturally, the labor office does not have to get back into an unpleasant situation, seeking credits and subsidies from the Federal State, in the words of a politician of the German Social Democratic party.
On Tuesday it was made public that the rate of inflation with the explosion of prices in the food sector and the oil derivatives sector reached the highest level for 13 years.
Comparing prices to last year, these are increasing by 3 per cent.
Venus: Twin Brother of the Earth.
In spite of its atmosphere it is not suitable for life, Venus is the planet most similar to the Earth, something that until now had not been proposed. The European probe "Venus Express" discovered rays in the atmosphere and swirls at the poles, the same ones that also appear in winter on Earth.
Already for three decades the researchers had the suspicion that rays could exist on Venus.
"But now the European probe has brought new evidence: it discovered electromagnetic ripples on the edge of the atmosphere. On Venus the pleading rays of a height of 54 kilometers from one cloud and behind the other, Cris Russell of the University of California in Los Angeles told the scientific journal "Nature."
According to the famouflaged model of Urey-Miller &apos; s lifetime, the rays caused life on Earth: storms may have provoked reactions in the ocean that, for their part, would have given rise to the amino acids that are the basic components of life.
On Venus it's not like this: if there was one day to live there, that would have to be in one of the early states of evolution," explains Sean Solomon of the Carnegie Institution in Washington.
There can be no life on the surface of Venus today: the atmospheric pressure is almost 100 times greater than on the Earth and temperatures of 500oC recur on the surface, oscillining in such a way that temperatures below zero can be reached.
"Possibly, Venus is an "diabolical" twin on Earth, but he's still a twin," Russell said.
The main objective of the "Venus Express" mission is to answer the question of why Venus, so similar to the Earth because of its mass and size, has evolved so differently.
Since there had been no special missions since the nineties, only for research from this planet, the launch of "Venus-Express" in November 2005 was a very important step for Venus's research.
The latest results of the investigations to be presented in many "Nature" articles, according to the researchers, highlight not only the uniqueness of Venus in the Solar System.
"That also shows that neighboring planets have more common characteristics with the Earth than we had assumed," ESA reports.
Despite well-known tempestuous stokes at the North Pole, the researchers also discovered in the southern polar region a vortex formed by the fast volving cloud.
This phenomenon is reminiscent of wind flows in the high terrestrial atmospheric layers of the poles, as described by Giuseppe Piccioni researchers from the Italian Institute of Special Astrophysics and Space Physics.
The 2nd Planet of the Solar System has the mass almost equal to the Earth and with its equatorial diameter of 12,000 kilometers is almost as large.
This planet also called daw dawn star or an evening stint orbit around the sun for nearly 225 days.
But its period of rotation is slower: a day in Venus equals 243 days on Earth.
Although Venus with its surface temperatures of 460 degrees and its atmospheric pressure of 92 bar does not resemble the Earth much, the scientists depart from the basis that both planets had formed under the same conditions.
On the two planets there had been agglomerated water on the Earth that stayed in the oceans, but in Venus it was dissected in the universe.
While carbon dioxide on Earth had been converted into carbon stones, in Venus it forms most of the atmosphere.
Koehler defended Merkel &apos; s rally with Dalai Lama.
Koehler, the Federal President, insists on the end of the fight between the parties for the Dalai Lama reception at the Federal Chancellery.
He indirectly criticized the German Social Democratic Party for its attacks on the Federal Chancellor after the rally in September.
Horst Koehler, the Federal President, worries about Germany's reputery and that's why he demanded that the grand coalition put an end to that fight for Dalai Lama's reception.
"In this case we shouldn't present ourselves as a divided nation," Koehler told "Handels Blatt newspaper."
At the same time Koehler expressed his understanding of the Dalai Lama's reception by Ángela Merkel (Christian Democratic Union Party) on Sept.
"Although I have great respect for the difference between cultures, human rights are absolutely valid for me," Koehler said.
"Everyone knows that Germany has to act with a great deal of foreign policy, the Federal President stresses, defending Merkel for the reproach she received in carrying out a new policy with China.
"The Federal Chancellor didn't take the distance from the traditional course of One China policy.
China also knows it," Koehler said.
Frank-Walter Steinmeier (Social-Democratic Party) Foreign Minister had indirectly expressed his displeasure at the Dalai Lama reception and was attacked by members of the Union.
Steinmeier criticised the Union for its non-operational foreign policy.
Dalai Lama &apos; s reception at the Federal Chancellery provoked great misunderstandings in Chinese-German relations.
Thus, the Chinese government not only withdrew the invitation to Peer Steinbrueck (Social Democratic Party), Germany's Minister of Finance, but also, according to information sources of the magazine "Spiegel," froze the so-called strategic dialogue of the Ministries of Foreign Affairs of the two countries.
Wen Jiabao, China's Head of Government, called on Federal Chancellor Angela Merkel to recognize this Dalai Lama reception as a mistake.
Affirming the position of China, Wen Jiabao said that China would not accept that European politicians would receive the religious leader of the Tibetans in its official position.
Dalai Lama &apos; s actions and words showed that he is trying to divide Tibet and the other Tibetan region of China, which now also includes other provinces.
Schaeuble warns that there will be riots such as those that occurred in France.
Battles with the police in the streets, shooting, burning cars: Wolfgang Schaeuble warns that there will be unrest as was the case in France.
The Federal Minister demands that every effort be made to ensure that social dissatisfaction in Germany does not move to the streets.
Barricads were barricaded, vandals shot the police with rifles: after serious riots by young people in France, the Federal Minister for Home Affairs, Wolfgang Schaeuble (Party of the Democratic-Christian Union) fears similar trends in Germany.
"We have to see ALSO that the social problems of our country don't take the violent form."
"That's what we've got to take seriously," he said in Hannover's "Neue Presse" newspaper.
Not all young people unwrite in the constant change of social reality.
The federal state, the federal states and the municipalities are already working with an emphasis to solve these problems, Schaeuble said.
That refers not only to the integration of immigrants.
"From the Integration Summit to the Conference of Islam: all this is already bearing its first fruit."
It's still too early to take for granted the end of the alarm.
At the same time Schaeuble pointed out that the German suburbs do not have the same building and sociological problems as in France.
The youths staged serious riots on the streets with the police north of Paris over the past few nights.
In Tolouse cars and public buildings were also burned.
Kenan Kolat, the President of the Turkish Community in Germany, demanded greater efforts from the Federal Government for integration.
Kolat, in view of the unrest in France, warns of an imitating effect.
It is true that the situation in Germany isn't as explosive as in France.
In spite of everything, actions are necessary.
Kolat pointed out that the rate of unemployment among young people of foreign origin under the age of 25 is above the average.
There needs to be a need to deal with more vocational training positions.
Kolat stresses: "We have to give a perspective to young people."
The president of the Turkish Community added that in Germany more than in France "there is great social control in the families," greater than the French country.
In addition, most young people of foreign origin do not want to destroy what their parents had built here.
Kolat says: "But in spite of everything, we have to be careful."
It is necessary to "give an opportunity for young people to receive a better job or vocational training."
Meanwhile the overnight in the Paris suburb of Villiers-le-Bel passed peacefully.
Hundreds of cops were involved in the action, a beaconed helicopter flew over the territory.
The responsible authorities reported three arrests, as well as garbages and burnt cars.
Since the previous day a team of almost 1000 police officers have been dealing with security.
After searching a suburb, Michelle Alliot-Marie, the Minister for Home Affairs, announced that police will take rigorous action until it is necessary.
About 80 police officers were injured by riots in Villiers-le-Bel on Monday night Tuesday.
Disruptions continued the next night in Tolouse.
The disturbance began in a traffic accident on Sunday, in which two young men were killed.
According to information provided by the prosecutor &apos; s office, the deaths of the young people were not because of the police.
