For the first time Musharraf governs without uniform.
One day after his resignation as head of the army, the Pakistani president was sworn in as president.
That's the end of the eight-year-old military government in Pakistan.
Pervez Musharraf, Pakistan's president, was swearing at an o'clock for a five-year second term on Thursday.
Pakistani television broadcast the ceremony live at the Presidential Palace in the capital Islamabad.
Musharraf vowed to respect the constitution and act for the well-being of the nation.
On Wednesday he transferred his mandate as head of the army to his confidant Ashfaq Kiyani and thus accepted international pressure, especially from the United States.
Eighteen years after he took power with an unblooted military coup, the ex-general rules the state for the first time as a civilian boss.
Upon his resignation as head of the Armed Forces, Musharraf fulfilled the main status of the opposition and the west.
The other requirement is an end to a state of emergency, which was proclaimed by Musharraf on November 3.
According to Malik Muhammad Qayyum, the state's attorney-in-chisman general, Musharraf's swearing-in will soon quan't quadruple the state's state's second-minute condition.
Since he was a state of emergency in the Supreme Court there have been plenty of Musharraf's pro-Trust jurists. Last week the country's Supreme Supreme Court rejected all the appeals ahead of the controversial new government leadership election on October 6.
Meanwhile, in an attack on a military convoy in the North's Watirstan region, five soldiers were killed and four others were injured.
An army spokesman bomb exploded on one of the streets on the border with Afghanistan.
Krautrock's founders come back.
The most famous German band of the 1970s is active: three members of "Harmonia" participated in a concert in Berlin.
A copy of "Live 1974" that was written in the band's finest era.
Already at that time they were very celebrities in foreign countries.
The attendance of these three lords at an official meal caused the sensation.
Each of them has in front of him his little box, his wires and above all his buttons.
In the middle is Dieter Moebius, who is 63 and dress dressed in a white shirt.
To his left is 57-year-year-old Michael Rother, who has his laptop and guitar.
On the right, watching from the auditorium, there is Hans-Joachim Rodelius, 73, who is very busy with the control panel.
Each contributes with their sounds, among them there is even rhythm.
Sometimes, without any intention, these sounds become a piece of music.
It doesn't seem to bother the lords.
Something very exciting for Krautrock's enthusiasts was the fact that the Harmonia band made music again.
What needs to be rebellious here is this: the precise questions have shown that the name of the band rumbling in people's minds, but it wasn't something so special that after 32 years the audience would miss her so much.
But why cann't Germans also clap their living legend of the 1970s?
Especially because Krautrock was always more valued in England and the United States rather than in his own country.
When the entertainment industry awarded an "Echo" music award to the members of Cologne's Can 2003 band for their work of life, in the room you could see the surprised faces of German pop artists.
And when Red Hot Chili Peppers thanked La Dusseldorf und, they all opened their mouth with astonishment.
Later Michael Rother spent his time on stage during the Red Hot Chili Peppers gig as if he had been an old friend (from the band).
Ignorance in your country and recognition abroad are just one thing.
It is a problem to remember this band, because the trio only existed for two years and at that time it did not achieve a great effect on the public, however, they obtained it later thanks to the others.
Some time ago Moebius and Roedelius had made music with artist Conrad Schnitzler and they had a name, Cluster, who promised a lot of fame.
At the beginning they played in the basement of Berliner Schaubuehne.
Later, for economic reasons, they worked with a music company in Duesseldorf that was patronized by a church.
For a time, Rother had a job in Kraftwerk and then worked as a guitarist in the band I achieved to improve the fame of this German band that at the time was too radical.
In 1973, Rother followed Cluster's musicians in exile to Forst in Weserbergland.
There in Alten Weserdorf, a 16th-century wretch, they lived as refugees from the city.
The way of life was less ideological than in Common I, where Roedelius could complain about his position.
That was more of an artist's life full of surprises.
They say one day an Ottenstein's Harmonia singing association banner showed up in the trash, which turned out to be a good name for art.
There were also drawbacks in using the Harmonia name.
"Musik von Harmonia" was called "Musik von Harmonia" and the second "De Lux."
Even though the music was quiet and simple, the band had financial problems.
Life as an artist wasn't just to get to know yourself.
The CD "Live 1974" was recorded during a concert in Griessem. Someone recalls that almost 50 people participated in this recording.
Then Rother expressed a desire to propulse Harmonia and do from time to time rehearsal.
For Roedelius and Moebius this demand is against customs.
In 1975 the band announced their separation.
Rother ended up on themes of esotericism and secured its existence with albums such as "Flammende Herzen."
Cluster revived the eighties and since that time has been more successful than Harmonia had.
Groenemeyer's live footage of Album in Griessmer has to be thanked.
His music company Grienland set himself the goal of cultivating his own German contribution to preserve it as living music.
Like Roedelius's work, the band also was again undiscovered.
Harmonia &apos; s appearance as a band takes place at the Casa de las Culturas del Mundo in Berlin.
The band was subsidised with public measures from the Wordtronics festival.
There's Julian Cope, the Englishwoman, the old punk and the author of the "Krautrock Sampler" manual.
Also present is Dr. Motte, the inventor of Loveparade.
In this way, all of Krautrock's influences on the next pop music are present.
Harmonia takes the position of a super-gang, a position that was empty.
Brian Eno's phrase accompanied by special music fills the room: "This music is the only true music, it belongs to the future."
There's pictures where Harmonia appears with growers on a farm.
The history of legend development is shown in black-and-and-wheel-white photo projections, where instruments with cables can be seen.
In the background there are three veterans who started improvising again.
They do nothing more than to attach the microphones to the organ and the battery in order to produce extraordinary tones with the help of the effects devices they had built with their own hands.
Nowadays e-channel music is how it had to be.
Three people delving into the technique.
That doesn't change anything.
Under the direction of Bárbara Morgenstern, in a musical vibe you cough, you whisper and in a good moment of emphasis you shoot "Aaaaaaaah."
Krautrock was always a term of English origin.
But it's never about rock.
It did not seek the origin of the genre in the blues, but in music schools (Can), of technologies (Kraftwerk) or fleeing artistic and social conventions (Cluster).
Everyone here screams "68" is also right, but now Harmonia keeps calm.
On the stage there is a very good reflection on what is already evident.
Where does it start and where does the music end?
That can tear one's nerves or give away quick lucky moments.
Richard Gere only lacks more cynicism.
The two "The Hunting Party" and "Moerderischer Frieden" are again dedicated to war in the Balkans.
But they both didn't have much conviction.
Either the concepts look a lot at actions and shooters or the two films are too exciting.
Richard Gere with his "The Hunting Party" can't help in this case either.
At the beginning there was some good news: Karadzic and Mladic, the war criminals, continue to hide in the mountains of the Balkans, but precisely there, in the dark interior of the country during the Bosnian war, actor Richard Gere was rediscovered.
We had finally lost sight of the handsomest man of the eighties, because no one wanted to see him after his pleasant and minor roles in women's films and after many press conferences on Buddhism.
Suddenly, Richard Gere is the most important plot to see perhaps the film "The Hunting Party - when a hunter becomes what he pursues" (Why nowadays the titles of the films have to be so long and crazy?).
The film has a marvellous presentation for view: deep down, his role as a reporter in the war is the presentation of what Richard Gere could do, if the destination and the film industry hadn't been so merciful to him. He was a man whose best times of life have already passed, who as a monument of the ruin of his decendency so far preserved most of his charm.
"The Hunting Party," in spite of this triumph, along with "Moerderischer Frieden" which is the German production and will also be presented this week, turns out to be only a series of unsuccessful films about the war in the Balkans.
One can narrate the film "Moerderischer Frieden" in a nutshell: they are two German soldiers in Kosovo (Adrian Topol, Max Riemelt) who want to save a Serb from Kosovo snipers, one of them acts very quickly and that also leads him to fall in love.
The meaning of the film is lost to actions and shootings, even though we celebrate that the Serbs aren't once again presented as the only culprits.
The film is like the German series "Alarm Fuer Cobra 11" without a motorway, but in a situation of war.
In the example of "The Hunting Party" you can investigate, because there really was never a good film about the war in the Balkans.
At another moment, with another director and another script author (Richard Shepard on two charges), the story about American reporters in the war leaving to detect a Serbian war criminal called "fox," it could probably be a new "Apocalypse Now."
Thus, the stories are surprisingly similar: the small teams twice go to the mountain to carry out something that the army can't do and every time it's a crazy journey.
One reason for the failure of "The Hunting Party" is when at the beginning we are introduced to Simon Hunt, who isn't allowed to be simply a heart-hearted cynic.
No, the script also envisions a loving adventure with a Muslim boss they'll kill next.
Then "The Hunting Party" is a long way from reality, especially when in the last five minutes reporters with an astonishing speed achieve what NATO, the CIA and all the head hunters of the world could achieve for five years, which was capturing the war criminal and leaving Muslim Bosniaks to avenge themselves in a fair way.
It's so wrong that they want to appeal to the international court in The Hague based on crimes against kinematic logic.
However, the purpose of the film is, in reality, a deeply human one.
The film wants to condemning the fact that Karada and Mladi are still free, and stresses that the Americans and the French aren't really preoccupied with chasing them, because they have political arrangements between them.
Sometimes the good and the bad ones are so similar, so a very famous poem of Brecht comes to mind.
In this poem it is a mask of an Asian demon: "In the great wrinkles of his forehead I see, sharing the feelings with him, how hard it is to be bad."
Movies such as "The Hunting Party" there's also these demons, but they show how tough it's to be good.
The problem is not only the genre of war films in the Balkans.
He recently published in a newspaper that all films about Iraq produced in Hollywood are fiascos.
It can also be added that there has never been a good film about South Africa.
It is almost always worth a rule: when a star gets into a region of crisis or the minefields, then something like "Rangoon" appears with Patricia Arguette, "On the Other Side of All Borders" with Angelina Jolie or "Bloody Spaniators" with Leonardo DiCaprio.
Possibly all of these conflicts occurred within a short period of time.
They've got to spend at least a few decades until Hollywood rolls a good holocaust movie taking as an example the film " Schindler's List."
Some movies already need more time. Classics such as the Vietnam War plays "The Deer Hunter," "Apocalypse Now" or "Full Metal Jackett" at the time weren't unanimously celebrated like masterpieces.
Nowadays maybe we know too much about global conflicts, and that's why in our time every carnage (Massenmord) is presented directly on two televisions at home.
And that's because script writers don't have the slightest hesitation (that's necessary to be able) to narrate good stories.
"To be or don't" or "The Great Dictator" proved so good at the end, because Lubitsch and Chaplin still didn't know how bad the Nazi regime was actually.
Maybe you shouldn't be a nice person to shoot a movie.
Maybe you have to be like the novelist Lawrence Norfolk with his sarcastic and distant gaze of an artist.
When he was asked about the parallels between his historical novel "Diccionario de Lempriere" and the war in the Balkans, he replied: "That's only because some things don't ever change."
The great cities will always be in jeopardy, men are always going to be cruelly killed and in Yugoslavia the war will always reign.
Von Beust gets the negative critique of the Olympic Games in response.
The mayor of Hamburg received much criticism from politics and economics for not requesting the Summer Olympic Games.
Many of them want to keep fighting for racing in the water sports section.
But Beust recalls being realistic in estimating the odds.
Hans-Joerg Schmidt-Trenz, managing director of the Chamber of Commerce in Hamburg, on Wednesday described the resignation as "not a good one for the economic site."
In the last time no other project in the big city fostered as much motivation as the idea of organizing the Olympic Games.
"Without this goal you wouldn't be able to maintain the political impulse at the same level."
Schmidt-Trenz demanded that the senate do not forget this project, but to find a new long-term application "that refers to another time."
On Wednesday Mayor Ole von Beust maintained his statements that Hamburg's ambitions in organizing the Summer Olympic Games would not take place until at least the year 2028.
"Hamburg doesn't decide on the request for the Olympic Games, it is decided by the German Olympic Sports Federation," says von Beust at WELT ON LINE.
He referred to the decision of the Federation of Sport (Sportbund) which is expected in early December on Munich's application for the 2028 Winter Olympic Games.
"That preference means to us that in this period of time, that until now we planned to have until 2028, from a realistic point of view we will have no opportunity."
"Despite everything, this dream didn't end forever," von Beust said.
The Hamburg Sports Federation (HSB) laments von Beut &apos; s statements.
This gives the impression that over the next 25 years Hamburg doesn't want to apply for the Olympic Games.
"It's too early to let the opportunity be removed, if at the moment the plans are wrecked," Gonter Plo, the director of HSB, said Wednesday.
Renunciation of the request is contradictory to the declaration of the head of the senator he made at a reception at the general meeting of the general meeting of the German Public Sports Federation of Higher Schools (adh).
There von Beust designated Hamburg's request for Universities 2015 not as "a replacement" for the Olympic Games in Hamburg.
Only last last week the Federation of Higher Schools Sport appointed Hamburg as a candidate for the application.
The Fraternation of the German Social Democratic Party (SPD-Bergerschaftsfraktion) reproached the senate for not taking advantage of such valuable time for the construction of houses in Hamburg.
"For many years the senate lashed with the development of the small Grasbrook neighborhood, because this was in plans for the Olympic Games or the construction of houses," said Carola Veit and Jan Quast, deputies of the German Social Democratic Party (SPD).
While the SPD always required integrated Olympia planning for small Grasbrook and then the use of built houses, the CDU stressed the importance of entertainment.
At the time, Hamburg's request for the 2012 Summer Olympic Games provoked great enthusiasm.
But Hamburg failed because of Leipzig.
It's not the wooden boxes.
Those who relate the terms of Sweden and a wooden box think of Ikea, but don't think about Andy Warhol.
- Isn't it?
Currently the wooden boxes produced in Sweden are like the shade that weighs on Warhol's list of works.
Most of the ninety-eight brillo-Box "type" on this list are fake.
After a few months ago the Swedish newspaper "Expressen" cast doubt on Andy Warhol's sunshine-Swimming authenticity of 1968, Stockholm's Museet Modern Museet Museum conducted internal investigations and reached disappointing conclusions: the six "Green-Boxes," which former museum director Pontus Hultén gifted to the Museet Modern Musseet museum are fake, and almost a hundred of the other "Stupid type," which are in circulation in the international art market, too.
Pre-history is too complicated with a confusing relationship between artistic and sponsored productions.
In 1964 Warhol created in New York his first "Girls-Boxes," he painted the wooden boxes of red, blue and white, with the same sample that they had dust cartons of soap powder "Green."
According to Warhol, at the exhibition of his 1968 works at the Museet Modern Museum, hundreds of glimpses cardboard boxes were exhibited. They were not made by Warhol (because of the expenses), but were brought directly from the "Brillo" cardboard factory.
It wasn't about works of art, it's about specimens (which were just objects) on display.
In the same year, in 1968, Warhol managed to produce a glimpse glimpse in Sweden, but they were not exhibited at the Modern Museet Museum.
Jessigories of those times now speak that almost fifteen boxes, which at that time have entered the history of art and the auctions as the original "Green-Boxes" of "Stupid" authorized by Andy Warhol.
At auction some of them were bought for more than $100,000.
The problem is that after the faculty director of the Pontus Hultén museum he made false statements that in 1968 in Sweden almost one hundred of the wooden boxes that Warhol left to him were exhibited.
But in reality, 105 boxes were produced in the same year 1990 in Malmo for Hultén and for the exhibition in St.Petersburg.
By this time Warhol had already died for 3 years.
Lars Bystrom, the head of the curators of the Modern Museet museum, researched six Brillo-Boxes owned by the museum and also three of Sweden's private property that were manufactured in 1968 in Stockholm. "Everything was imprinted and scrubbed their squeezed silvers and then painted them to oil with the brush.
On the contrary, the structure of the 1990 copies shows that the painting was applied with an unimpressed revelel directly the tight silvers. In addition, it is not the oil painting, but the acrylic paint.
"These boxes have not been authorized by the artist and must be removed from Andy Warhol's official Brillo-Boxes list," says Lars Nittve, the director of the Museet Modern museum, adding that if The "Andy Warhol Art" Authenology Commission in New York won't reach any conclusions, the Museet Museum will classify these six boxes as copies/objects of exhibition."
Already in Juliance The Authentication Commission formally announced that it accepts these "serious allegations" in which they are soap powder boxes.
But from that moment there everything remained in absolute silence.
Rebel soldiers surrender in Manila.
Revolving soldiers who had stuck in a hotel in Manila have surrendered in the Philippines and demanded the fall of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
Leader Antonio Trillanes, who reproaches the president, corruption and electoral fraud, told a reporter she couldn't answer for spilling blood.
Soldiers loyal to the government had raided the hotel using tear gas.
Nearly two hundred soldiers have been entrenched in the building for a few hours.
Almost half of them, among them Trillanes, were on trial on Thursday for attempting a coup d'état in 2003 that ended in failure.
He took advantage of the negotiations, to suddenly be able to settle with about 30 sympathizers.
Evidently the military cops who had to keep an eye on him had moved into their ranks and followed his mutiny.
They walked into the "Peninsula" hotel and demanded the fall of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
U.S. government troops blockaded the neighborhood around the Noble Hotel.
The president called for an urgent session of the Security Cabinet.
Norberto Gonzales, the national security adviser, condemned the soldier's behavior.
"People are fed up," Trillanes said.
"Let's summon people to move into our ranks".
U.S. Danilo Lim, who previously ran the elite Scout Reangers troops, said the other military troops soon wanted to continue the march.
Major government critics on Thursday called for a large demonstration against the president.
For months his government has received reproach for corruption.
In 2003, Manila's financial district was a site that attracted full attention for the mutiny that was attempted against Arroyo.
The uprising flared after about 24 hours because the army wanted to participate.
Since 1986 there have been at least seven coup attempts in the Philippines against the democratically elected government.
U.S. Internet will be Versatel's largest shareholder.
U.S. Internet secured more than 20 per cent the shares of telecommunications company Versatel.
On Wednesday the company reported that the average price for one share is 14, 46 euros.
It has only 20.05 per cent of the share capital.
There was a lot of speculation about the company's absorption.
There's also the options to further increase the financial part at Versatel.
United Internet reported that with this step it wants to accelerate the consolidation of the German DSL (Digital Subscriber Line).
On Wednesday they already made the assumptions about entry into the business, after the Brendenberg bank increased its stake to Duesseldorf's network operator by 5 per cent to 15.1 per cent.
In the financial market this means that the financial institution keeps the bonds of participation for a third-party partner.
Already in the day the previous day it took almost 10 per hundreds to telecommunications company Drillisch.
Drillisch on the capital-enhancing measure placed 17,4 million of the new shares with a value of 106,4 million euros.
U.S. Internet has bought 5.15 million shares for 31.4 million euros.
In addition, the companies began negotiations on the marketing partnerships of DSL-products.
"Let us strengthen our collaboration," the United Internet spokesman explained.
The two companies founded an  MSP company to acquire competitor Freenet.
On Thursday the United Internet reported that at the beginning, the two companies want to be happy with the participation of 10 per cent to Freenet and don't make use of their rights, i.e. they involve 18.49 for hundreds of Freenet.
This meant that both companies maintained all the options, especially the participation of the public limited company Freenet.
Negotiations between United Internet and Freenet on a strategic partnership ended in mid-November.
This "game" with Freenet that will last for many months and isn't very comprehearted is part of the consolidation of Telecom's German market.
At the end of the third trimester Freenet had 2.9 million customers with a mobile phone contract and $1.27 million with a DSL contract or a full contract.
Until now the United Internet and Drillisch holding it directly had 6.01 per cent stake in Freenet.
Then they had agreed that Drillisch will achieve the other four percent.
The right to buy 18.49 per cent of the investment company Vatas was already agreed in August.
